from pathlib import Path
import hashlib
import json
import sys
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
PLAN=Path(__file__).resolve().with_name('model-candidates.json')
plan=json.loads(PLAN.read_text()); candidates={c['id']:c for c in plan['candidates']}; sources={s['id']:s for s in plan['sources']}; rows={}; enrich={}
if '--check-inputs' in sys.argv[1:]:
 inventory=json.loads(PLAN.with_name('approved-candidates.json').read_text())
 assert len(candidates)==len(plan['candidates'])==52
 assert len(sources)==len(plan['sources'])==33
 assert [dict(id=c['id'],action=c['action'],family=c['family']) for c in plan['candidates']]==inventory['entries']
 assert all(c['family'] in {f['id'] for f in plan['families']} for c in plan['candidates'])
 assert all(a['id'] in sources for c in plan['candidates'] for a in c['sourceAnchors'])
 assert inventory['approvedPlan']==PLAN.relative_to(ROOT).as_posix()
 assert inventory['approvedPlanSha256']==hashlib.sha256(PLAN.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
 print(json.dumps(dict(status='pass',plan=PLAN.relative_to(ROOT).as_posix(),candidates=len(candidates),families=len(plan['families']),initialSourceAnchors=len(sources),approvedInventoryMatches=True,planHashMatches=True,writes=0),indent=2))
 sys.exit(0)
def add(id,objective,description,mechanism,example,latex,variables,assumptions,question,options,tradeoff,mode='worked example',pre=None):
 c=candidates[id]; rows[id]=dict(id=id,objective=objective,description=description,mechanism=mechanism.split(' || '),example=example,latex=latex,variables=[dict(symbol=a,meaning=b,unit=u) for a,b,u in [v.split('|') for v in variables.split(';')]],assumptions=assumptions,question=question,options=options,tradeoff=tradeoff,mode=mode,pre=pre if pre is not None else c['prerequisites'])
add('data-quality-dedup-audit','Distinguish exact duplicates, approximate matches and evaluation contamination using explicit document families.',
'Duplicate detection defines an equivalence or similarity rule over content; it does not directly measure whether that content is true or useful. Raw-byte identity, normalized-text identity and overlap between token shingles can produce different groups. A group must be assigned to a split before its members are sampled, otherwise paraphrases or mirrors can leak across a nominal document split. Removing repeated data also changes exposure and source balance. A false merge discards distinct information, while a missed duplicate can overrepresent one source. The appropriate audit records both kinds of error on reviewed examples and measures downstream effects at a fixed token budget.',
'Preserve original bytes and source identity before normalization. || Compute exact equality and a declared shingle similarity separately. || Form document-family groups, inspect borderline pairs, and assign whole groups to train, development or final. || Count unique retained families, mistaken merges and cross-split matches with separate denominators.',
'Authored documents A and B have shingle sets {red, square, left} and {red, square, right}. Their intersection has 2 items and union 4, so Jaccard similarity is 0.5. A threshold of 0.5 merges them even though left and right encode a different answer. Two other files with identical normalized text but different whitespace are exact normalized duplicates. Of 12 input files, retaining 9 representatives means a 25% file reduction; it says nothing by itself about token reduction or correctness.',
 r'J(A,B)=\frac{|A\cap B|}{|A\cup B|},\qquad r_{\rm files}=1-\frac{N_{\rm kept}}{N_{\rm input}}',
'A,B|Sets of declared shingles|set;J|Jaccard overlap for a nonempty union|dimensionless;N|Document counts at the specified boundary|documents;r_files|Removed-file fraction|dimensionless',
'Nonempty shingle union; exact set arithmetic. Threshold and authored semantic labels are teaching choices, not a universal deduplication rule.',
'Why can a 0.5 similarity threshold remove useful information in this example?', ['The shared shingles hide the answer-changing left/right distinction.','Jaccard overlap proves the two statements have the same meaning.','Removing a quarter of files proves a quarter of tokens were removed.'],
'Looser matching catches more repeated material but can erase rare distinctions. Stricter matching retains more variety and more missed duplicates.')
add('data-mixture-sampling','Calculate expected exposure per source and distinguish it from one finite seeded draw.',
'A training mixture is a probability distribution over available data, not a statement that every unique token is seen once. A small source can receive more expected epochs than a large source even when it receives fewer total draws. Sampling with replacement permits repeats and omissions. Sampling without replacement within an epoch has a different cursor contract. Changing mixture weights changes the learning objective in expectation; it also changes which domains consume a fixed budget. Report unique pool size, total draws, probabilities, replacement policy and tokenizer identity together.',
'Count the unique available tokens under one tokenizer. || Allocate a fixed draw budget by source probability. || Divide expected draws by pool size to obtain expected epochs. || Inspect realized source counts and repeated examples for a specified RNG seed; do not replace them with their expectation.',
'Pool A contains 100 unique tokens and B contains 20. For 240 token draws with weights 0.75 and 0.25, expected draws are 180 and 60, hence expected exposures are 1.8 and 3 epochs. Under independent source selection the standard deviation of A draws is sqrt(240×0.75×0.25)=6.708. An observed 174/66 allocation is possible and does not alter the configured 3:1 probabilities.',
 r'\mathbb E[D_i]=B p_i,\qquad E_i=\frac{B p_i}{U_i},\qquad \operatorname{Var}(D_i)=B p_i(1-p_i)',
'B|Total independent token draws|tokens;p_i|Source selection probability|dimensionless;U_i|Unique tokens in source i|tokens;D_i|Realized source draws|tokens;E_i|Expected equivalent passes|epochs',
'Independent source draws with replacement, positive pool sizes and probabilities summing to one. These are exposure calculations, not predicted learning curves.',
'Which pool receives more expected passes?', ['B receives 3 expected passes despite only 60 expected draws.','A receives more passes because its pool is larger.','Both receive exactly one pass.'],
'Upweighting a small high-value source can strengthen its signal while increasing repetition and reducing the budget available for other domains.')
add('sequence-packing-loss-denominators','Draw document and target masks, then reconstruct the loss denominator of a packed batch.',
'Packing places multiple sequences into fixed storage rows. Padding utilization, permission to attend across documents and permission to train on a target are three distinct choices. A causal mask blocks future inputs; a document mask can additionally block earlier tokens from another packed document. A target mask decides which shifted next-token predictions contribute to the objective. These masks need not be identical. Treating padding or the first token of an unrelated document as an ordinary target can change the objective even when the row has the expected shape.',
'Write each document with its boundary marker and shift input/target positions explicitly. || Mark causal visibility and optional same-document visibility separately. || Exclude padding and any deliberately excluded boundary targets. || Sum all included losses before dividing by the total number of included targets.',
'One sequence contributes two valid target losses, 1 and 3 nats; another contributes five losses of 1 nat each. Token-mean loss is (1+3+5)/7=9/7=1.285714 nats. Averaging sequence means gives (2+1)/2=1.5 nats. Packing the rows into one buffer does not justify switching denominators. If a row has eight slots but only seven valid shifted targets, dividing by eight reports 1.125 and understates the objective.',
 r'L=\frac{\sum_{b,t}m_{bt}\ell_{bt}}{\sum_{b,t}m_{bt}},\qquad A_{ij}=\mathbf 1[j\le i]\mathbf 1[d_j=d_i]',
'L|Mean included target loss|nats per target;m_bt|Target inclusion flag|dimensionless;ell_bt|Negative log probability of one target|nats;A_ij|Optional causal same-document visibility flag|dimensionless;d_i|Document identity at input position i|identifier',
'Positive valid-target count; the displayed attention formula chooses document isolation. Other packing policies must declare their different boundary semantics.',
'Which denominator preserves the stated global token objective?', ['Seven included targets.','Two sequences regardless of length.','Eight storage slots including excluded positions.'],
'Packing can reduce wasted storage while making boundary semantics harder to inspect. High utilization is not evidence of correct masking.')
add('synthetic-data-selection-feedback','Track a generated example through verification and selection without converting acceptance into ground truth.',
'Synthetic supervision has a lineage: a generator produces a candidate, a verifier or judge assigns a signal, and a selection rule decides whether to train on it. Each stage can introduce a different bias. Repeating a generator’s answer does not create independent evidence, and selecting only accepted outputs can hide the verifier’s blind spots. Generation seeds, prompts, model identities and verifier versions therefore belong to the dataset record. Final evaluation needs independently specified cases and scoring rules, rather than the same filter that selected training material.',
'Retain rejected candidates as an audit sample alongside accepted examples. || Evaluate an independent reference on a bounded problem where the answer is actually computable. || Separate selection rate, precision among accepted examples and coverage of problem types. || Compare the trained model on a reserved task family; selection statistics are not model-quality measurements.',
'Four authored candidates answer 2+2: 4, 5, 4 and 6. A faulty verifier accepts even numbers, so it accepts three candidates, including the wrong 6. Selection rate is 3/4; correctness among accepted candidates is 2/3. Deduplicating identical problem/answer pairs leaves {4,6}, whose correctness is 1/2. This denominator change reveals why duplicate correct answers can make a selected corpus look more reliable.',
 r'\operatorname{precision}_{\rm selected}=\frac{N_{\rm correct\,and\,selected}}{N_{\rm selected}},\qquad \operatorname{selection\ rate}=\frac{N_{\rm selected}}{N_{\rm generated}}',
'N|Counts under the stated candidate or unique-pair boundary|examples;precision_selected|Independent correctness among accepted candidates|dimensionless',
'Explicit arithmetic reference and deterministic faulty filter; positive denominators. This is a worked selection audit, not a synthetic-data training simulator.',
'What does the accepted answer 6 demonstrate?', ['The filter accepts some incorrect answers, even though its rule is deterministic.','Deterministic verification guarantees mathematical truth.','A high selection rate proves generalization.'],
'A restrictive verifier can improve accepted precision while reducing diversity. A permissive or biased verifier can amplify generator errors through repeated training.')
add('data-coverage-language-slices','Compare source slices under explicit byte, token and task denominators.',
'Token count depends on both text and tokenizer. Different scripts and source formats can consume different token budgets for comparable tasks; byte length introduces another encoding-dependent denominator. Neither a low token count nor a low token-normalized loss alone establishes better understanding. A coverage audit preserves source and language labels, inspects retention after filtering, and measures quality on declared task slices. FineWeb’s documented English filtering is a scoped recipe, not evidence that its thresholds fit every language or community.',
'Record the exact UTF-8 text and tokenizer version for each source slice. || Count retained documents, bytes and tokens separately. || Compute loss and downstream success within slices before aggregating. || Change a mixture only after stating whether the goal is token exposure, task coverage or a quality threshold.',
'The authored strings "cat" and "猫" each occupy 3 UTF-8 bytes, but a character tokenizer emits 3 and 1 tokens respectively. This is a coding example, not a comparison of linguistic meaning. In a separate audit, slice A retains 80/100 documents and B retains 4/10. Overall retention is 84/110=76.36%, which conceals B’s 40% retention. Reporting only the overall value would miss that imbalance.',
 r'r_s=\frac{N_{{\rm kept},s}}{N_{{\rm input},s}},\qquad \bar\ell_s=\frac{\sum_{t\in s}\ell_t}{T_s}',
'r_s|Retention within source slice s|dimensionless;N|Document count|documents;T_s|Included targets under one tokenizer|tokens;ell_t|One target negative log likelihood|nats;bar ell_s|Mean slice loss|nats per token',
'Authored strings and slice counts; UTF-8 and a fixed character tokenizer. Cross-tokenizer loss values require further normalization and do not directly rank language ability.',
'Which observation is concealed by the overall 76.36% retention?', ['Slice B retained only 40% of its documents.','Both slices retained 76.36%.','Three UTF-8 bytes always mean three tokens.'],
'Aggregate scores simplify comparison but can conceal minority-source failures. Slice metrics expose them at the cost of smaller, noisier denominators.')
add('reverse-mode-autodiff','Accumulate every branch contribution to a reused parameter and check it by finite differences.',
'A forward computation creates intermediate values. Reverse-mode differentiation traverses the dependency graph backward, multiplying each incoming derivative by the local derivative and adding contributions where paths meet. Reusing one parameter in several operations creates several paths to the loss; its final gradient is their sum. The optimizer acts only after these derivatives have been computed. This distinction lets a learner locate an incorrect derivative independently of an incorrect update rule. Numerical finite differences provide a separate local check, although their accuracy depends on step size and smoothness.',
'Save the forward operands required by local derivative rules. || Seed the scalar loss derivative with one. || Traverse operations in reverse dependency order and add, rather than overwrite, shared-input gradients. || Compare selected parameter derivatives with central differences while holding every other input fixed.',
'Let u=wx and L=(u+w)^2/2. At w=2 and x=3, u=6, u+w=8 and L=32. The path through u contributes 8×3=24 to dL/dw; the direct w path contributes 8, giving 32. Treating the second path as a replacement would give 8, while omitting it would give 24. For this quadratic, [L(w+h)-L(w-h)]/(2h) is 32 in exact arithmetic for any nonzero h.',
 r'\bar v_i\mathrel{+}=\bar v_j\frac{\partial v_j}{\partial v_i},\qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial w}\approx\frac{L(w+h)-L(w-h)}{2h}',
'v_i,v_j|Intermediate scalar values|dimensionless;bar v_i|Accumulated derivative of loss with respect to v_i|loss per value;w|Reused trainable scalar|dimensionless;h|Finite-difference perturbation|dimensionless;L|Scalar objective|dimensionless',
'Smooth scalar graph and nonzero finite-difference step; floating-point cancellation can limit very small h. Lifecycle tape executes these mechanisms for all 423 parameters.',
'What is the correct derivative with respect to w?', ['32, because both computational paths contribute.','24, because only the multiplication path is differentiable.','8, because the later path overwrites the earlier one.'],
'Retaining intermediates reduces recomputation but consumes memory. Gradient checks are expensive if run on every parameter of a large model.', 'executable core')
add('tiny-causal-decoder','Join tensor shapes, causal probabilities, gradients and optimizer state into one reproducible trained model.',
'The bounded lifecycle model is a complete causal decoder rather than a collection of supplied-gradient formulas. A nine-symbol lookup feeds one width-six attention head and a width-twelve nonlinear block. Residual paths carry activations around each sublayer; normalization controls their local scale. A separate output head predicts nine next-token probabilities. Every learned coefficient participates in the same reverse-mode graph. Fixed positions, masks and tokenizer are part of the computation but are not trainable parameters. The exact small architecture is a teaching choice, so its behavior must be measured on its own documented symbol task.',
'Initialize all learned weights from the saved seed; keep fixed positions and normalization convention separate. || Trace embeddings through causal Q/K/V mixing, residuals, tanh features and output logits. || Form target losses, backpropagate through all eleven tensor groups, and apply the selected optimizer. || Compare reserved copy and swap cases with a train-fitted baseline and preserve the entire experiment state.',
'The exact parameter ledger is 9×6 embedding + 4×6×6 attention projections + (6×12+12+12×6+6) feed-forward + (6×9+9) output =423. Fixed positions and gain-free RMS normalization add no parameters. At binary64 payload size the weights occupy 423×8=3384 bytes, excluding runtime objects and activations. In the default tested run, 320 actual updates produce 8/8 copy development answers; this finite toy result is not language competence.',
 r'P=Vd+4d^2+(dh+h+hd+d)+(dV+V)=423',
'P|Learned scalar coefficients in this declared decoder|parameters;V|Vocabulary size, 9|tokens;d|Hidden and single-head width, 6|features;h|Feed-forward width, 12|features',
'One head, separate input/output weights, two feed-forward biases, output bias, fixed sinusoidal positions, gain-free RMS normalization, tanh activation and binary64 arithmetic.',
'Why are the fixed positional vectors absent from the 423 count?', ['They affect the forward computation but are not updated as learned parameters.','They are ignored by causal attention.','All vectors in a model are necessarily trainable.'],
'A small model permits complete inspection and exact tests. Its narrow vocabulary and task leave most real language phenomena outside the evidence.', 'executable core')
add('causal-pretraining-objective','Construct shifted next-token targets and interpret token likelihood separately from answer accuracy.',
'Autoregressive pretraining asks a model to predict each next token from earlier inputs. The target is the shifted sequence, not the token already visible at the same position. Summing negative log probabilities gives a sequence score; averaging included targets gives the training objective. Exponentiating this mean yields perplexity under the same tokenization and mask. Answer accuracy is a different measurement: it observes selected decisions rather than every token probability. A model can become better at punctuation or repeated format markers while its answer decisions remain unchanged.',
'Pair input position t with token t+1 and mark valid targets. || Compute stable log-softmax and gather each target’s log probability. || Sum losses and divide by included targets across the batch. || Fit a simple baseline on training data only and compare the same reserved targets.',
'For three target probabilities [1/2,1/4,1/2], total NLL is ln2+ln4+ln2=ln16=2.772589 nats. Mean NLL is 0.924196 nats and perplexity is 16^(1/3)=2.519842. A uniform nine-token predictor has mean NLL ln9=2.197225 and perplexity 9. Feeding the answer token at the very position being scored would invalidate this comparison through target leakage.',
 r'L=-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{t:m_t=1}\log p_\theta(x_{t+1}\mid x_{\le t}),\qquad \operatorname{PPL}=e^L',
'L|Mean included next-token NLL|nats per token;M|Included target count|tokens;m_t|Target inclusion flag|dimensionless;p_theta|Conditional target probability|dimensionless;PPL|Perplexity under this tokenization|dimensionless',
'Natural logarithms, positive target probabilities and nonzero denominator. Perplexities with different tokenization/masks are not directly comparable.',
'Which comparison uses the same next-token objective?', ['Model and baseline scored on identical shifted targets and masks.','Model answer accuracy versus baseline perplexity.','Training loss after showing the target at its own input position.'],
'Token likelihood supplies dense supervision but weights frequent formatting and rare task-critical targets according to their actual counts.', 'executable core')
add('optimizer-schedule-stability','Separate gradient clipping, adaptive moments, decay and the learning-rate schedule.',
'The derivative specifies a local direction; the optimizer and schedule specify the actual state transition. Global norm clipping rescales an entire gradient vector before the update. Adam additionally carries first and second moments and corrects their initialization bias. AdamW applies a distinct shrinkage term rather than feeding an L2 derivative into those adaptive moments. A schedule changes the step size over updates, and its position belongs in a restart record. The lifecycle core executes clipping and constant-rate Adam or SGD; warmup, decay schedules and AdamW below are worked extensions, not hidden features of that core.',
'Compute the raw gradient norm before any clipping. || Apply one declared global scale to all gradient entries. || Update optimizer moments and their time counter from the clipped gradient. || Apply the stated learning rate and, only when selected by the algorithm, a separate decay term; compare fixed-seed runs.',
'For raw gradient [3,4], norm is 5. A clip threshold of 1 gives [0.6,0.8]. With initial weights [2,2] and SGD rate 0.1, the result is [1.94,1.92]. In a separate scalar AdamW example with weight 2, adaptive direction 1, learning rate 0.1 and decay coefficient 0.01, the result is 2−0.1×1−0.1×0.01×2=1.898. That example uses the common learning-rate-scaled decay convention explicitly.',
 r'g_c=g\min\!\left(1,\frac{c}{\lVert g\rVert_2}\right),\qquad \theta^+=\theta-\eta u-\eta\lambda\theta',
'g,g_c|Raw and globally clipped gradient vectors|loss per parameter;c|Gradient norm limit|loss per parameter;theta|Parameter vector|dimensionless;eta|Step size in the declared numerical convention|dimensionless;u|Adaptive update direction|dimensionless;lambda|Decoupled decay coefficient|dimensionless',
'For zero gradient use clipping multiplier one. The second equation declares one AdamW convention; lifecycle core sets decay to zero and has a constant schedule.',
'What preserves the direction of gradient [3,4] during norm clipping?', ['Multiplying both entries by 1/5.','Clamping each entry independently to 1.','Replacing the gradient with [1,0].'],
'Clipping can contain extreme updates but can also suppress useful signal. Stability on a small run does not determine a universally good schedule.', 'executable clipping; worked schedule and AdamW')
add('microbatch-equivalent-updates','Recover a full-batch gradient from unequal target counts before clipping or stepping.',
'Gradient accumulation is a way to evaluate one global objective through several smaller forward/backward passes. Equivalence requires the same parameters for all those passes and the same weighting of included targets. Averaging two microbatch means equally silently changes the objective when their target counts differ. Optimizer steps between microbatches change the parameters at which later derivatives are evaluated. Nonlinear operations such as norm clipping also fail to commute with accumulation. Randomness, stateful layers and rounding add further limits to exact equivalence.',
'Freeze the current weights across all microbatches in the update. || Retain each microbatch loss sum and valid-target count. || Add gradient sums and divide once by the global target denominator. || Clip the resulting global gradient once, then make exactly one optimizer step.',
'Two valid targets yield mean scalar gradient 3; five yield mean gradient −1. The global mean is (2×3+5×(−1))/7=1/7. Equally averaging microbatch means gives 1 and is wrong. If each local mean is clipped to magnitude 1 first, the weighted result becomes (2−5)/7=−3/7, even reversing the sign. At weight 2 and SGD rate 0.7, the correct single update produces 1.9.',
 r'g=\frac{\sum_k n_k g_k}{\sum_k n_k},\qquad \theta^+=\theta-\eta\operatorname{clip}(g)',
'g_k|Mean gradient for microbatch k|loss per parameter;n_k|Valid targets in microbatch k|tokens;g|Global token-mean gradient|loss per parameter;theta|Parameter evaluated consistently across microbatches|dimensionless;eta|SGD step size|dimensionless',
'All gradients evaluated at the same weights with the same stochastic choices needed for equivalence. Exact arithmetic example; finite reductions can introduce rounding differences.',
'When should clipping occur to reproduce the declared full-batch update?', ['After target-weighted accumulation and before the single optimizer step.','Once per microbatch before summation.','After two separate optimizer steps.'],
'Microbatches lower activation demand at the cost of extra scheduling and stricter state bookkeeping. They do not automatically preserve the objective.')
add('model-lifecycle-stage-contract','Classify a transition by checkpoint, data, loss, trainable state, optimizer policy and evaluation.',
'Names such as pretraining, mid-training and post-training summarize recipes but do not uniquely specify a mathematical operation. A useful stage contract identifies the starting checkpoint, example distribution, objective, parameter subset, optimizer state policy and acceptance measurements. Continued language modeling can change only the data mixture while preserving the same next-token objective. SFT can preserve the decoder while changing target masks. DPO changes the training signal to relative chosen/rejected likelihoods against a reference. Inference changes activations and selected outputs without necessarily changing learned weights.',
'Record a checkpoint identity and tokenizer/config identity before branching. || State which data and targets enter the loss, and which parameters may change. || Declare retained or reset optimizer/RNG/schedule state. || Compare acquisition, retention and failures on development cases before exposing a final test.',
'A base checkpoint contains 423 trainable weights. A continuation branch retains all 423, its Adam moments and RNG but changes the copy/swap sampling distribution. An SFT branch also retains 423 trainable weights but uses only two response targets per sequence. A rank-one adapter on a separate 4×6 matrix would train 10 factors instead of 24 base entries, but its objective could still be SFT, DPO or another differentiable loss.',
 r'\mathcal C=(\theta_0,\mathcal D,\mathcal L,\mathcal T,\mathcal O,\mathcal E)',
'C|Stage contract tuple|record;theta_0|Starting checkpoint|parameters;D|Training data distribution|distribution;L|Objective and denominator|function;T|Trainable parameter subset|set;O|Optimizer RNG and schedule policy|state contract;E|Evaluation and retention gates|protocol',
'Contract notation is an authored organizational tool, not a universal naming standard. The tuple does not itself train a model.',
'Which change alone makes a continuation stage a different objective?', ['Replacing token NLL with a specified preference-pair loss.','Giving the unchanged loss a new stage name.','Saving the same weights under a different filename.'],
'Stage names aid communication, while explicit contracts take more effort and reveal incompatibilities that a name can conceal.', 'executable stage branches')
add('domain-adaptive-continuation','Measure new-domain acquisition and old-domain retention from the same starting checkpoint.',
'Continued pretraining resumes parameter learning on a new distribution, often using the same language-model objective as the starting model. It differs from retrieval or prompting, which can change available context without changing weights. Domain adaptation research includes masked-language-model examples as well as causal decoder recipes; one should not label every cited experiment autoregressive. In the bounded lifecycle case, copy and swap use the same prompts with incompatible answers. This deliberately severe shift exposes the difference between learning a new rule and preserving an old one.',
'Save the base checkpoint and evaluate both domains before adaptation. || Draw only training examples from the declared new-domain mixture. || Retain or explicitly reset optimizer state according to the stage contract. || Measure both domain losses and answer accuracy at the same checkpoint after the fixed budget.',
'For one binary prompt, suppose a model assigns probability 0.9 to the old answer and 0.1 to the new answer. Old/new NLLs are 0.105361 and 2.302585 nats. If adaptation changes these probabilities to 0.2 and 0.8, the losses become 1.609438 and 0.223144. New-domain loss improves by 2.079442 while old-domain loss worsens by 1.504077. These probabilities are an authored worked example; the studio reports its own computed values.',
 r'\Delta L_A=L_A(\theta_{\rm after})-L_A(\theta_{\rm before}),\qquad \Delta L_B=L_B(\theta_{\rm after})-L_B(\theta_{\rm before})',
'L_A,L_B|Held-out losses under fixed domain-specific targets|nats per target;theta|Checkpoint being evaluated|parameters;Delta L|After-minus-before loss change|nats per target',
'Same evaluation cases, masks and tokenizer before/after; negative loss change is improvement. Binary illustrative probabilities do not describe every vocabulary class in the core.',
'What does a positive old-domain loss change mean here?', ['The model assigns less likelihood to the old held-out targets.','The new domain necessarily improved.','No parameters changed.'],
'Concentrated adaptation can acquire a new distribution quickly but may trade away previous behavior. Retrieval has a different state and evaluation boundary.', 'executable continuation')
add('replay-retention-forgetting','Reason about a fixed-budget retention tradeoff without treating replay as a guarantee.',
'Replay mixes old training examples into adaptation. It changes the expected objective and consumes part of the same update budget. Comparing a longer replay run with a shorter new-only run confounds mixture with computation. Retention must be evaluated on reserved old-domain examples, not merely on repeated training items. In the lifecycle task the same prompt has opposite desired answers in copy and swap. No deterministic single-answer policy without a domain identifier can satisfy both simultaneously. That impossibility is a property of this constructed task, not a universal claim about continual learning.',
'Hold the starting checkpoint, total draws and optimizer policy fixed. || Select a declared old-domain replay probability. || Record realized domain draws rather than assuming the expected fraction occurred exactly. || Compare both reserved-domain losses, and explain whether conflict or insufficient acquisition limits the result.',
'For one conflicting binary prompt let p be the old-answer probability and q the old-example mixture fraction. Expected NLL is −q ln p−(1−q)ln(1−p), minimized at p=q. With q=0.25, old/new losses at that optimum are ln4=1.386294 and −ln0.75=0.287682 nats. Greedy output selects the new answer, so old accuracy is zero even though replay has raised old-answer probability above zero.',
 r'L(p)=-q\log p-(1-q)\log(1-p),\qquad p^*=q\quad(0<q<1)',
'p|Probability of the old-domain answer|dimensionless;q|Old-domain mixture weight|dimensionless;L|Expected binary target loss|nats per target;p*|Optimal probability for the specified conflicting prompt|dimensionless',
'One identical prompt with contradictory binary labels, unlimited fitting of its probability and no domain tag. Finite decoder optimization need not reach this ideal optimum.',
'Why can replay improve old-answer likelihood without restoring old-answer accuracy?', ['Probability can rise while remaining below the greedy decision threshold.','Likelihood and accuracy are the same statistic.','Replay guarantees both incompatible labels are always correct.'],
'Replay allocates scarce updates to retention; it can reduce new-task progress and cannot solve an information-theoretic label conflict by itself.', 'executable replay; worked ideal optimum')
add('long-context-adaptation','Separate accepted context length, causal correctness, memory growth and demonstrated long-distance use.',
'A model accepting a longer input is only the first part of context extension. Positional representations must remain meaningful over new distances, masks must still enforce causality, and training/evaluation must test whether earlier evidence is actually used. YaRN studies a particular rotary-position extension method; its mechanism is not the fixed sinusoidal position rule of the 423-parameter core. Retrieval at long distances, distractor resistance and instruction following can fail separately. Increasing the storage limit alone establishes none of those abilities.',
'Declare original and extended positional configurations and the data used for adaptation. || Test future-token invariance independently of task correctness. || Move a required symbol to different distances and compare an evidence-removed control. || Inventory KV elements at each length with explicit head, layer and dtype conventions.',
'With two layers, two KV heads, four features per head and two bytes per stored element, a full KV cache uses 2×layers×tokens×heads×features×bytes. At 128 tokens it is 8192 bytes; at 512 it is 32768 bytes. This fourfold storage increase follows from the declared shape. If a 512-token interface still answers only 5/10 far-evidence cases, accepted length 512 is not evidence of successful long-context use.',
 r'B_{KV}=2LTH_{KV}d_hb',
'B_KV|Key plus value payload|bytes;L|Layer count|layers;T|Stored token positions|tokens;H_KV|KV head count|heads;d_h|Features per KV head|features;b|Stored bytes per element|bytes per element',
'Uncompressed full KV payload with no sharing or overhead. This lesson is a worked extension; the lifecycle core has at most eight input positions and does not implement YaRN.',
'Which observation establishes storage capacity but not learned long-distance use?', ['The system accepts 512 tokens and allocates the corresponding KV payload.','Answers change correctly when the required far-away evidence changes.','A reserved long-distance task is solved under a fixed scoring rule.'],
'Longer contexts consume storage and attention work; positional adaptation and useful information retrieval require their own evidence.')
add('checkpoint-averaging-boundaries','Check parameter alignment before averaging and evaluate the resulting function.',
'Weight averaging combines numbers at matching parameter coordinates. Shape equality is necessary but does not prove semantic alignment: hidden units can be permuted while preserving the original function. Independently trained models can therefore disagree about which coordinate represents which feature. A shared initialization and compatible training recipe make an average more interpretable, but do not guarantee that every task is preserved. An averaged checkpoint also needs an explicit optimizer policy; averaging weights does not establish suitable Adam moments or a resume cursor.',
'Check architecture, tokenizer, parameter order and source checkpoint identities. || Form a declared weighted average only across compatible coordinates. || Evaluate both original checkpoints and the average on identical reserved cases. || Treat averaged weights as a new model identity and explicitly choose fresh or otherwise justified optimizer state.',
'Two equivalent two-unit linear networks represent y=x with incoming vector [1,−1] and outgoing [0.5,−0.5], or with both vectors permuted to [−1,1] and [−0.5,0.5]. Each dot product is 1. Averaging corresponding incoming and outgoing entries gives zeros, so the averaged network produces y=0. By contrast, averaging compatible scalar weights 1 and 3 gives 2; that arithmetic alone still does not predict task quality.',
 r'\bar\theta=\sum_k a_k\theta_k,\qquad \sum_k a_k=1,\quad a_k\ge0',
'theta_k|Coordinate-aligned candidate weights|parameters;a_k|Averaging weights|dimensionless;bar theta|New averaged checkpoint|parameters',
'Compatible parameter coordinates and tokenizer required. Permutation counterexample is an exact linear network; no averaging feature is executed by the lifecycle core.',
'Why do equal tensor shapes fail to guarantee a useful average?', ['Equivalent functions can use different hidden-unit coordinate assignments.','Equal shapes imply identical learned features.','Averaging always preserves each source prediction.'],
'Averaging may combine nearby solutions without storing an ensemble at inference, but it can also create a poor function and ambiguous optimizer history.')
add('sft-chat-loss-masks','Identify exactly which shifted response targets contribute to supervised fine-tuning.',
'Supervised fine-tuning supplies desired outputs for prompts. The model can receive both prompt and response tokens during teacher forcing while the objective trains only selected response targets. Seeing a prompt as context does not require predicting every prompt token in the loss. Role delimiters, assistant starts and end-of-turn markers require an explicit template convention; an off-by-one mask can train the wrong role or omit the terminating token. The executable lifecycle example uses one authored answer marker and supervises the answer plus EOS. Multi-turn templates are a worked extension, not an implemented chat framework.',
'Render the exact template and retain role ownership for every token. || Shift the target sequence before placing the loss mask. || Include each desired assistant target and the declared end marker; exclude prompt-only targets. || Divide by supervised targets and compare gradients under an intentionally wrong mask.',
'For a six-target dialogue, prompt-target losses are [2,3,1,2] nats and response-target losses are [0.4,0.6]. All-token mean is 9/6=1.5 nats. Assistant-only mean is 1/2=0.5 nats. The prompt tokens still influence the response through attention, so their embeddings can receive gradients even though their own target losses are masked. A target mask is not a promise that all prompt-path parameters remain frozen.',
 r'L_{SFT}=-\frac{\sum_t m_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid x,y_{<t})}{\sum_t m_t}',
'L_SFT|Masked supervised target loss|nats per included target;m_t|Assistant-target inclusion flag after shifting|dimensionless;x|Prompt context|tokens;y_t|Supervised response target|token identifier;p_theta|Conditional target probability|dimensionless',
'Positive included-target count and a stated role/template boundary. Core mask covers one answer and EOS; multi-turn masking here is worked-only.',
'Can prompt embeddings receive gradients when prompt target losses are zeroed?', ['Yes, because response predictions depend on prompt representations.','No, target masking freezes every parameter used by the prompt.','Only if the prompt is incorrectly used as its own target.'],
'Response-only supervision focuses the direct objective while still relying on prompt representations. Template errors can silently change what is learned.', 'executable one-turn response mask')
add('preference-reward-model','Compute a pairwise preference probability and distinguish a learned score from verified correctness.',
'A reward model can assign scalar scores to candidate responses and fit observed pairwise preferences through their score differences. Only differences determine the pairwise probability; adding the same constant to both scores changes neither the prediction nor the loss. The labels can express style, usefulness or a chosen rubric, so a high score is not automatically factual truth. The reward model has its own train/development split and failure modes. It is distinct from the policy that generates answers and from a deterministic checker with an explicitly computable target.',
'Record prompt, chosen response, rejected response and the rubric that produced the label. || Compute both scores and their difference. || Fit a logistic pairwise loss, retaining gradients for both responses. || Evaluate held-out pairs and inspect order, length and source-support confounders before using scores as rewards.',
'If chosen score minus rejected score is ln3, the chosen probability is 3/(1+3)=0.75 and loss is −ln0.75=0.287682 nats per pair. The derivative with respect to the difference is 0.75−1=−0.25. With learning rate 0.1 applied directly to that scalar difference, it rises by 0.025. Scores [5+ln3,5] yield exactly the same preference probability as [ln3,0].',
 r'p(w\succ l)=\sigma(r_w-r_l),\qquad L=-\log\sigma(r_w-r_l),\qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial(r_w-r_l)}=\sigma(r_w-r_l)-1',
'r_w,r_l|Chosen and rejected scalar scores|dimensionless;p|Predicted pairwise preference probability|dimensionless;sigma|Logistic function|function;L|Pairwise negative log likelihood|nats per pair',
'Bradley–Terry logistic score-difference model with one explicit preference label. No learned reward model is implemented in the lifecycle core; its reward stage uses a disclosed checker.',
'What remains unchanged after adding 5 to both response scores?', ['The pairwise preference probability and loss.','Only the absolute scores remain unchanged.','The preference label must reverse.'],
'Pairwise judgments can be easier to collect than absolute scores, but reward calibration outside the comparison distribution requires separate evidence.')
add('direct-preference-objective','Compute DPO from policy and frozen-reference response likelihoods with an explicit pair denominator.',
'DPO changes the policy directly using chosen and rejected response likelihoods relative to a reference. The reference supplies a fixed baseline for the ratio; it must not silently follow each policy update. Response log probability normally sums the included autoregressive token log probabilities. Pair averaging and token averaging are different objective choices. Preference labels specify which answer to favor, but they do not prove truth or guarantee that the highest-probability token in the full vocabulary becomes correct. The lifecycle implementation exposes the actual policy/reference scores and independent task accuracy.',
'Freeze a reference checkpoint at the declared branch point. || Score chosen and rejected responses under both models with the same response-token convention. || Form the difference of log-ratios, scale by beta and apply the negative log-sigmoid loss. || Backpropagate only through policy scores; evaluate held-out response behavior separately.',
'For an authored two-response case, policy probabilities are chosen 0.6 and rejected 0.4; reference probabilities are 0.5 and 0.5. At beta=1 the log-ratio difference is ln1.5=0.405465, logistic value is 0.6 and loss is −ln0.6=0.510826. If policy equals reference the difference is zero and loss is ln2 regardless of the reference preference. Swapping labels reverses the difference and raises this example’s loss to −ln0.4=0.916291.',
 r'L_{DPO}=-\log\sigma\!\left(\beta\left[\log\frac{\pi_\theta(y_w\mid x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_w\mid x)}-\log\frac{\pi_\theta(y_l\mid x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_l\mid x)}\right]\right)',
'L_DPO|Loss for one labeled preference pair|nats per pair;pi_theta|Current policy response probability|dimensionless;pi_ref|Frozen reference response probability|dimensionless;y_w,y_l|Chosen and rejected responses|token sequences;x|Prompt|token sequence;beta|Log-ratio scale|dimensionless',
'Positive probabilities, fixed reference and consistent response token scoring. Core uses authored swap preferences and answer-plus-EOS scores, not collected human labels.',
'Which state receives gradients in the declared DPO branch?', ['The current policy; the branch reference remains fixed.','Both policy and reference follow the same update.','Only the reference is trained.'],
'DPO avoids fitting and sampling from a separate reward model in this update, but remains dependent on the preference data and reference choice.', 'executable DPO')
add('policy-gradient-kl-control','Separate sampled advantages, old-policy ratios, clipping and reference KL.',
'A policy-gradient update learns from actions sampled under a recorded policy. The score-function derivative weights log-probability gradients by an advantage; the sampled action and its assigned reward are treated as data for that update. PPO adds a clipped probability-ratio surrogate for reusing rollout data near the old policy. The old rollout policy and a longer-lived reference policy play different roles. Clipping the surrogate is not a hard guarantee that every new probability ratio or KL stays bounded. The lifecycle core instead implements a two-action REINFORCE update with an exact detached baseline and categorical KL; PPO is worked here only.',
'Retain the identity and action probabilities of the policy that generated each rollout. || Compute a stated return/baseline advantage and detach it for the score-function term. || For PPO, compare current/old ratios with the clipping interval and respect the sign of the advantage. || Compute reference KL under its explicit distribution and check actual task return independently.',
'For a sampled action with old probability 0.5, current probability 0.7 and advantage +2, ratio is 1.4. With epsilon 0.2, PPO’s contribution is min(1.4×2,1.2×2)=2.4. For advantage −2 it is min(−2.8,−2.4)=−2.8. The sign determines which incentive is clipped. Separately, KL([0.7,0.3] || [0.5,0.5]) is 0.082283 nats, not 0.2 and not the ratio 1.4.',
 r'J_{clip}=\min(rA,\operatorname{clip}(r,1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)A),\quad r=\frac{\pi_\theta(a\mid s)}{\pi_{old}(a\mid s)},\quad D_{KL}(p\Vert q)=\sum_i p_i\log\frac{p_i}{q_i}',
'J_clip|One PPO surrogate contribution to maximize|reward units;r|Current to rollout-policy action ratio|dimensionless;A|Detached advantage|reward units;epsilon|Surrogate clip width|dimensionless;p,q|Current and reference distributions for this KL direction|probabilities;D_KL|Categorical divergence|nats',
'One sampled-action term with fixed advantage. PPO formula is worked-only; executable core uses REINFORCE plus exact two-action KL, not PPO or GRPO.',
'For advantage −2 and ratio 1.4, what is the clipped-surrogate contribution?', ['−2.8, because the minimum retains the worse contribution.','−2.4, because all ratios are always forcibly clamped.','+2.4, because reward signs are ignored.'],
'Reusing rollouts can reduce sampling demand while increasing sensitivity to stale policies and surrogate choices. A clipped objective does not replace measured policy drift.')
add('verifiable-reward-group-updates','Compute relative group advantages and identify what a verifier does and does not establish.',
'Verifiable reward replaces a learned preference score with an explicit checking procedure. The reward can still be wrong if parsing, tolerance or task specification is wrong. Group-relative methods compare several outputs for the same prompt; the group’s reward distribution provides a baseline and scale. A group in which every output has the same reward provides no relative distinction. That does not establish that the task was solved. R1’s group-relative recipe and Tülu’s PPO-based verifiable-reward recipe are different algorithms, while the lifecycle core uses a smaller REINFORCE bandit.',
'Sample a declared number of outputs with a saved policy/RNG identity. || Preserve raw outputs and verifier decisions, including parse failures. || Compute a stated group mean and variance convention; handle zero variance explicitly. || Apply the selected algorithm and score novel cases with an independently specified correctness rule.',
'For rewards [1,1,0,0], mean is 0.5 and population standard deviation is 0.5. Standardized advantages are [1,1,−1,−1]. Rewards [0,0,0,0] have zero variance; this worked convention returns zero advantages rather than dividing by zero. All four outputs can therefore be wrong while producing no within-group signal. The studio’s four sampled actions per prompt use an exact policy baseline instead of this group normalization.',
 r'\bar r=\frac1G\sum_i r_i,\quad s=\sqrt{\frac1G\sum_i(r_i-\bar r)^2},\quad A_i=\begin{cases}(r_i-\bar r)/s&s>0\\0&s=0\end{cases}',
'G|Outputs sampled for one prompt|outputs;r_i|Verifier reward|reward units;bar r|Group mean|reward units;s|Population standard deviation|reward units;A_i|Standardized relative advantage|dimensionless',
'Explicit population-variance and zero-variance teaching convention; real implementations may add epsilon or use another normalization. This group arithmetic is not the core bandit estimator.',
'What can an all-zero advantage group mean?', ['Every sampled answer was wrong under the verifier.','All answers must be correct.','The optimizer has proved the verifier sound.'],
'Group comparisons reduce dependence on a learned value baseline but can waste batches with no reward variation and amplify verifier mistakes.', 'executable checker; worked group normalization')
add('reward-proxy-overoptimization','Compare a rewarded proxy with independently scored task success and locate divergence.',
'An optimization signal defines what updates favor. If it rewards a correlated shortcut rather than the intended task, stronger optimization can increase the proxy while reducing usefulness. This can happen with a learned reward model or a deterministic rule. The lifecycle example makes the error concrete: the faulty verifier rewards every answer a even when the swap target is b. Its reward is fully inspectable, and correctness uses the independent target rule. A reward curve alone therefore cannot serve as the success metric.',
'Predeclare the task score independently of the optimized proxy. || Introduce one explicit verifier or rubric defect while preserving the same task cases. || Log sampled actions, rewards, baseline/advantage and actual correctness. || Compare checkpoints on the same reserved cases, including the slice where the proxy conflicts with the target.',
'On four authored questions, policy A gives three correct one-token answers and one wrong one-token answer. A length proxy of 0.1 per token gives mean reward 0.1 and accuracy 3/4. Policy B emits five tokens for every answer but gets only two answers right: reward rises to 0.5 while accuracy falls to 1/2. In the executable binary checker case, always choosing a can receive reward 1 on every prompt but only 1/2 accuracy on balanced a/b targets.',
 r'\Delta R_{proxy}>0\quad\not\Rightarrow\quad\Delta S_{task}>0',
'R_proxy|Mean optimized reward under its disclosed rule|reward units;S_task|Independent task success rate|dimensionless;Delta|After-minus-before change at a fixed evaluation boundary|difference',
'Authored counterexample and a separately specified task scorer. No claim that every reward model inevitably fails or that a particular real system followed this trajectory.',
'Which pair of observations demonstrates divergence?', ['Higher mean length reward together with lower independently scored accuracy.','Higher reward alone.','A larger number of generated tokens alone.'],
'Easy-to-compute proxies provide frequent feedback, while independently scored outcomes may be costly or delayed. Omitting the latter can hide optimization failures.', 'executable faulty verifier; worked length proxy')
add('low-rank-adaptation','Multiply a low-rank update and count trainable factors without confusing them with the training objective.',
'LoRA freezes a base matrix and expresses its update as a product of smaller trainable factors. Rank limits the directions available to that update, rather than declaring which behavior is trained. The same parameterization can be optimized under supervised, preference or other differentiable objectives. The base weights remain part of the forward computation and still need storage unless another representation changes that requirement. Factor count, optimizer state, activation memory and task quality are separate quantities. The 423-parameter lifecycle core currently trains all weights; it does not implement adapters.',
'Declare the base matrix shape, factor shapes, rank and scale. || Keep the base fixed while computing the factor product and adding the scaled update. || Differentiate through the factors only, then verify base entries are unchanged. || Compare task behavior and trainable state under a fixed objective and budget.',
'For W of shape 4×6 and rank 1, B=[1,2,0,−1]^T and A=[1,0,2,0,0,−1] have 4+6=10 trainable entries versus 24 base entries. At scale 1, the first row of BA is [1,0,2,0,0,−1] and the second is twice that. Input e3 (third coordinate one) receives update [2,4,0,−2]^T. This exact multiplication says nothing by itself about quality after training.',
 r'W=W_0+\frac{\alpha}{r}BA,\quad B\in\mathbb R^{d\times r},\quad A\in\mathbb R^{r\times k},\quad P_{adapter}=r(d+k)',
'W_0|Frozen base matrix|dimensionless;B,A|Trainable low-rank factors|dimensionless;d,k|Output and input widths|features;r|Adapter rank|rank;alpha|Declared adapter scale|dimensionless;P_adapter|Trainable factor count|parameters',
'Rank one, d=4, k=6 and alpha/r=1 in the example. Biases and any additional adapted matrices are excluded. Worked-only parameterization.',
'What does the number 10 count?', ['Trainable entries in the two rank-one factors.','All weights needed to run the base model.','The number of different objectives that LoRA supports.'],
'Low-rank factors reduce trainable optimizer state but restrict the update subspace. Lower trainable count is not a guarantee of equivalent task quality.')
add('teacher-student-distillation','Calculate soft-target loss and gradient, then evaluate the student separately from its teacher.',
'Distillation trains a student to match information supplied by a teacher. Soft probability targets retain relationships among alternatives that a single hard label discards. Temperature changes both distributions and derivative scaling; mixing soft and hard losses therefore requires a declared convention. A teacher’s generated explanation is a sequence target, while matching its class distribution is a different supervision signal. Neither is the same as quantizing stored weights. The student must still be evaluated on reserved tasks because teacher errors and transfer-set gaps can be inherited.',
'Record teacher identity, transfer inputs and whether targets are logits, probabilities or generated tokens. || Apply the same temperature convention to teacher and student for soft loss. || Compute the stated hard/soft objective and update only student parameters. || Compare reserved task quality, calibration and resource payload with independent denominators.',
'At temperature 1, teacher probabilities [0.75,0.25] and student probabilities [0.5,0.5] give cross-entropy ln2=0.693147 nats. The student-logit gradient is student minus teacher: [−0.25,0.25]. Replacing the teacher distribution with hard class one changes it to [−0.5,0.5]. At temperature 2 with the same softened probabilities, an unscaled soft loss has half that logit gradient; a T²-scaled convention multiplies it by four.',
 r'L_{soft}=-T^2\sum_i p_i^{(T)}\log q_i^{(T)},\qquad \frac{\partial L_{soft}}{\partial z_i}=T\big(q_i^{(T)}-p_i^{(T)}\big)',
'T|Common teacher/student temperature|dimensionless;p_i^(T)|Teacher softened target probability|dimensionless;q_i^(T)|Student softened probability|dimensionless;z_i|Student pre-temperature logit|dimensionless;L_soft|Temperature-scaled soft-target loss|scaled nats',
'Teacher probabilities detached; fixed shared temperature. T² scaling is explicit. Worked calculation only; no student distillation trainer is in the lifecycle core.',
'What information does [0.75,0.25] retain that a hard first-class label discards?', ['The teacher assigns nonzero relative support to the second class.','The exact teacher architecture and all its weights.','A guarantee that the first class is factually correct.'],
'A smaller student can be easier to serve but may lose behaviors absent from transfer data. Matching the teacher does not certify independent correctness.')
add('probability-calibration-abstention','Evaluate probability quality and selective error using distinct calibration and final-test data.',
'Confidence is a numerical prediction about outcomes, not a guarantee attached to a fluent answer. Calibration asks whether cases assigned similar probabilities succeed at corresponding frequencies under a stated task and distribution. Temperature scaling fits a scalar on development logits; it usually preserves the class ordering while changing probabilities. Abstention adds a decision policy: only answer when confidence passes a threshold. Lower error among answered cases can be purchased by answering fewer cases, so coverage belongs beside selective error. If no cases are answered, selective error is undefined rather than zero.',
'Fit any calibration parameter on development data only. || Score probability quality with an explicit Brier or log-loss convention. || Choose an abstention threshold before opening final cases. || Report answered count, error among answered cases and failures by slice, including empty denominators.',
'Binary probabilities [0.8,0.6,0.4,0.2] for class one with targets [1,0,1,0] have Brier score (0.04+0.36+0.36+0.04)/4=0.2. Predicted-class confidence is [0.8,0.6,0.6,0.8]. Threshold 0.75 answers two cases, both correct: coverage 1/2 and selective error 0/2. Threshold 0.9 answers none, so coverage is zero and selective error has no denominator.',
 r'B=\frac1N\sum_i(p_i-y_i)^2,\quad C=\frac{|A|}{N},\quad R=\frac{\sum_{i\in A}\mathbf1[\hat y_i\ne y_i]}{|A|}\ (|A|>0)',
'B|Binary one-probability Brier score|dimensionless;p_i|Probability of class one|dimensionless;y_i|Binary class-one label|dimensionless;A|Cases passing the confidence threshold|set;N|All evaluated cases|cases;C|Answer coverage|dimensionless;R|Error conditional on answering|dimensionless',
'Binary Brier convention shown, not sum over both class coordinates. Authored predictions; no calibration fitting feature is implemented in the lifecycle core.',
'How should a threshold answering zero cases be reported?', ['Zero coverage and undefined selective error.','Zero coverage and proven zero risk.','Perfect calibration.'],
'Abstention can reduce errors among answered cases while excluding difficult or underrepresented slices. Calibration learned on one distribution can fail after a shift.')
add('judge-rubric-agreement','Separate agreement, task correctness and preference under a reproducible judging rubric.',
'A judge turns an output and rubric into a score. A model judge, a human judge and an exact checker can disagree because they observe different evidence or optimize different criteria. Agreement measures consistency; it does not prove that the shared answer is correct. Pair order and output length can change judgments without changing factual content. A useful evaluation preserves the rubric, raw responses, order assignment and disagreements rather than collapsing them into one unexplained score. The examples below are authored labels, not records of recruited human participants.',
'Define correctness, source support and style as separate rubric dimensions. || Counterbalance pair order and retain each judgment with its evaluator identity. || Compare both agreement and correctness against the appropriate reference. || Review disagreements and shared errors rather than interpreting consensus as ground truth.',
'For four binary cases the reference is [1,1,0,0]. Judge A emits [1,1,1,0] and B emits [1,0,1,0]. They agree on cases 1,3,4: agreement is 3/4. A is correct on 3/4, B on 2/4. Their agreement on case 3 is a shared error. If the first response is preferred 7/10 before swapping order but only 3/10 afterward, content and presentation order need separate investigation.',
 r'G=\frac1N\sum_i\mathbf1[j_i^{A}=j_i^{B}],\qquad S_A=\frac1N\sum_i\mathbf1[j_i^{A}=y_i]',
'G|Observed pairwise judge agreement|dimensionless;S_A|Judge A correctness against the declared reference|dimensionless;j_i^A,j_i^B|Judge labels|category;y_i|Task reference label|category;N|Paired cases|cases',
'Authored binary labels and exact case matching; agreement here is raw agreement, not chance-corrected kappa. Human preference tasks may lack a single factual reference.',
'What does agreement on case 3 establish?', ['Both judges make the same error relative to this reference.','The reference must be wrong because two judges agree.','Agreement equals factual correctness by definition.'],
'Automated judges can scale scoring but can also share systematic biases. Human judgments require a clear rubric and do not become specialist review merely by being labeled human.')
add('retrieval-supported-generation','Track evidence retrieval, answer likelihood and claim support as separate stages.',
'Retrieval supplies candidate evidence to a generator. A retrieved document can be relevant without supporting the generated claim, and a supported statement can still fail to answer the question. A model may ignore supplied evidence or use a stale source. The RAG paper formalizes one family of probabilistic retrieval/generation models; not every application with search implements that marginalization. The atlas’s existing retrieval specimen is a deterministic scored-source baseline. This lesson adds worked model probabilities and support accounting, without presenting that baseline as a trained generator.',
'Preserve query, source version, retrieved ranking and the exact evidence spans. || State whether the generator conditions on one concatenated context or a mixture over documents. || Score each answer claim for support and question relevance independently. || Compare source removal, contradictory-source and irrelevant-but-true controls.',
'In a two-document mixture, retrieval weights are [0.7,0.3] and the probability of one complete candidate answer under the two documents is [0.8,0.2]. Its mixture probability is 0.7×0.8+0.3×0.2=0.62. Separately, an answer with two supported claims out of three has support fraction 2/3; that is not the probability 0.62. A statement that a server uses 400 W may be supported but irrelevant to a question asking how many servers are available.',
 r'p(y\mid x)=\sum_z p(z\mid x)p(y\mid x,z),\qquad S=\frac{N_{\rm supported\ claims}}{N_{\rm scored\ claims}}',
'x|Question or input|text;y|Complete candidate answer|text;z|Retrieved document in the declared finite mixture|document;p|Conditional probabilities under their stated models|dimensionless;S|Claim support fraction under the scoring rubric|dimensionless;N|Claims at the stated scoring boundary|claims',
'Exact normalized two-document teaching mixture; RAG top-k truncation can require a different approximation boundary. Support is rubric evidence, not model likelihood.',
'What does the value 0.62 represent?', ['The candidate answer probability under this declared document mixture.','The fraction of its claims supported by sources.','The probability every source is current and accurate.'],
'More retrieved material can improve evidence coverage while adding irrelevant or contradictory context. Generating citations is not equivalent to demonstrating support.')
add('test-time-search-verification','Distinguish finding a correct candidate from selecting one under a finite verification budget.',
'Inference can spend additional computation on multiple candidates or search without changing the model’s weights. A verifier then selects or rejects candidates under a task-specific rule. Pass-at-k asks whether a correct candidate exists in a sampled set; deployed selected-answer accuracy asks whether the system actually identifies one. A faulty verifier can discard the correct candidate or choose an attractive wrong one. Candidate dependence also matters: repeated near-identical samples do not provide independent chances. This differs from speculative decoding, whose acceptance correction preserves a sampling distribution rather than verifying answer truth.',
'Fix generation and checking budgets and retain all candidates, including failures. || Score candidate correctness independently of the selector when a reference exists. || Report both candidate-set success and selected-answer success. || Count model calls, generated tokens and verification calls separately; do not convert them into measured latency without a timing experiment.',
'If independent candidates each succeed with probability 0.25, the probability at least one of four succeeds is 1−0.75^4=0.683594. This is an independence calculation, not an observed model result. In a concrete four-candidate set [wrong, correct, wrong, wrong], pass-at-4 is one, but a selector choosing the first candidate has zero final correctness. A perfect existence score therefore can coexist with a failed answer.',
 r'P(\text{at least one success})=1-(1-p)^k',
'p|Single-candidate success probability under the assumed distribution|dimensionless;k|Independent candidates|candidates;P|Candidate-set success probability|dimensionless',
'Independent identically distributed success events for the probability calculation. Correlated samples and imperfect selectors invalidate interpreting this as final accuracy.',
'Can pass-at-4 be successful while the selected answer is wrong?', ['Yes; the correct candidate may exist but not be selected.','No; pass-at-4 proves the selector is correct.','Only if model weights were updated during sampling.'],
'Extra inference can improve the chance of finding useful candidates, but consumes computation and increases reliance on the verifier. It is not post-training unless parameters are updated.')
add('model-system-regression-contract','Attach an evaluation result to the complete system configuration and apply a stated rollback rule.',
'A useful output depends on more than model weights. Tokenizer, prompt template, retrieved sources, tool schemas, verifier, decoding settings and environment state can change behavior. A result without these identities cannot distinguish model improvement from a changed test harness. Regression testing keeps a stable case set and scoring contract while changing a declared component. Development feedback is used for iteration; a final set exposed during repeated choices becomes part of that feedback process. The lifecycle checkpoint identifies its own narrow model/data/config state, while a deployed system needs a wider record.',
'Freeze the evaluation cases, score rules and required system identities. || Change one declared component and retain the old configuration for comparison. || Score acquisition and protected failure slices at both identities. || Apply predeclared rollback thresholds and record the actual component change responsible for the comparison.',
'An authored baseline passes 18/20 task cases and all 4 source-support cases. A new retriever passes 19/20 task cases but only 3/4 source-support cases. Under the predeclared rule “task accuracy at least 90% and all four source checks pass,” the candidate fails despite higher aggregate task accuracy. The source-support count is a separate gate, not a fifth task silently mixed into a new denominator.',
 r'\operatorname{accept}=\mathbf1[S_{task}\ge\tau]\mathbf1[F_{protected}=0]',
'accept|Boolean acceptance under the declared policy|dimensionless;S_task|Task success fraction|dimensionless;tau|Minimum task success threshold|dimensionless;F_protected|Failures on explicitly protected cases|cases',
'Authored finite regression contract; thresholds are chosen before evaluation and do not certify open-ended model safety. System component identities must remain available.',
'Why does the candidate fail the stated gate?', ['One protected source-support case regressed despite higher task accuracy.','Every improvement in accuracy requires automatic acceptance.','Nineteen successes is lower than eighteen.'],
'A strict protected slice can block useful aggregate improvements, but silently changing its threshold after seeing results defeats the intended evidence contract.')
add('image-text-contrastive-learning','Compute a paired image/text similarity loss and test a shortcut with held-out compositions.',
'Contrastive image/text learning places both modalities into a comparable representation space and trains matched pairs to score above alternatives in a batch. It supplies a retrieval-like training signal rather than an instruction to generate a caption token by token. Normalization and temperature determine the similarity scale. Negative pairs are constructed from the batch and may include legitimate alternative captions, so label assumptions matter. A representation that recognizes red and square separately still needs evaluation on the previously unseen red-square combination. The lifecycle decoder does not train image encoders; this is a worked multimodal extension.',
'Encode each image and caption into vectors and state the normalization convention. || Form the full pairwise similarity matrix and identify the true paired diagonal. || Compute image-to-text and text-to-image cross-entropies separately, then their stated mean. || Compare correct retrieval on held-out compositions and controls that remove color or shape information.',
'For two authored paired examples, let the normalized similarity logits be [[1,0],[0,1]] at temperature 1. In either row or column, the matching probability is e/(e+1)=0.731059. Symmetric mean contrastive loss is −ln0.731059=0.313262 nats per directional example. If both images are encoded identically, no deterministic dot-product comparison can separate their captions without another distinguishing feature.',
 r's_{ij}=\frac{u_i^\mathsf T v_j}{\tau},\quad L=-\frac1{2N}\sum_i\left[\log\frac{e^{s_{ii}}}{\sum_j e^{s_{ij}}}+\log\frac{e^{s_{ii}}}{\sum_j e^{s_{ji}}}\right]',
'u_i,v_j|Unit-normalized image and text feature vectors|dimensionless;tau|Positive contrastive temperature|dimensionless;s_ij|Pair similarity logit|dimensionless;N|Paired examples in the batch|pairs;L|Mean symmetric directional cross-entropy|nats',
'Two uniquely matched pairs, unit vectors and temperature one in the example. False-negative captions and general image understanding are outside this exact arithmetic.',
'What does a low contrastive loss directly train?', ['The matched pair to receive high probability among the batch alternatives.','A complete autoregressive caption decoder.','Guaranteed understanding of every unseen composition.'],
'Paired supervision can use rich natural-language labels while inheriting ambiguity, source bias and shortcuts in the image/text pairs.')
add('visual-language-bridge','Trace visual feature dimensions into a language model and distinguish alignment from visual use.',
'A vision-language bridge converts image features into inputs with the language model’s required width. Shape compatibility allows the computation to run, but does not prove that the generated answer depends on the image. The trainable state can change across stages: one recipe may initially freeze the vision encoder and language model while fitting a projector, then update additional components for instruction following. The original LLaVA paper documents such a staged choice; other architectures differ. The worked matrix here exposes the interface without executing an image model.',
'Declare patch count, visual-feature width and language input width. || Multiply by a declared projection and preserve patch ordering. || Identify frozen and trainable components separately for each training stage. || Compare image swaps, blank-image inputs and text-only baselines on questions whose answers truly depend on the image.',
'Two patch rows Z=[[1,2],[3,4]] have visual width 2. With projector W=[[1,0,1],[0,1,−1]] of shape 2×3, visual tokens are ZW=[[1,2,−1],[3,4,−1]], now width 3. The projector has 6 trainable entries if it has no bias. Replacing both patches with zeros makes projected tokens zero, but a language prior could still produce a plausible answer; that would not establish image grounding.',
 r'H_v=Z_vW,\qquad Z_v\in\mathbb R^{N\times d_v},\quad W\in\mathbb R^{d_v\times d_l}',
'H_v|Projected visual tokens in the declared row-vector convention|dimensionless;Z_v|Patch feature matrix|dimensionless;W|Projection weights with no bias in this example|dimensionless;N|Patch count|patches;d_v,d_l|Visual and language widths|features',
'Authored fixed features and a linear projector, not an executed vision encoder or replication of an entire VLM. Matrix orientation is explicitly row-vector based.',
'Which observation tests visual use beyond mere shape compatibility?', ['Changing the image changes the answer correctly on an image-dependent question.','The projected tensor has the expected width.','The answer sounds plausible with a blank image.'],
'A lightweight projector limits trainable state but may constrain cross-modal alignment. Larger or jointly trained interfaces require new data and evaluation evidence.')
add('speech-audio-sequence-objectives','Relate audio samples, time frames and transcript errors without mixing their denominators.',
'An audio model receives sampled waveforms or derived features, while a transcript decoder predicts a sequence of text tokens. Sample rate, window length and hop determine the time representation; transcript labels need not align one-to-one with frames. Whisper uses a particular log-Mel representation and sequence-to-sequence task format. That is different from a CTC alignment objective or an audio-generation objective. Evaluation must also declare text normalization: case, punctuation and tokenization can change edit counts. The example below computes geometry and word errors without claiming to execute speech recognition.',
'Retain waveform provenance, sample rate and task labels. || Compute frame boundaries under a stated padding/window convention. || Distinguish audio frames, text tokens and reference words. || Score substitutions, deletions and insertions against a fixed normalized reference, with speaker/noise slices separate.',
'A one-second waveform sampled at 16000 Hz contains 16000 samples. With a 400-sample window, 160-sample hop and no padding, frame count is 1+floor((16000−400)/160)=98. A centered/padded library can differ. Reference “turn left now” and hypothesis “turn right now please” have one substitution and one insertion over three reference words, giving WER=2/3. Insertions can make WER exceed 100%; it is not a bounded accuracy percentage.',
 r'F=1+\left\lfloor\frac{N-W}{H}\right\rfloor,\qquad WER=\frac{S+D+I}{N_{ref}}',
'F|Unpadded full-window frame count|frames;N|Waveform length at declared sample rate|samples;W,H|Window length and hop|samples;S,D,I|Substitution deletion and insertion counts|word edits;N_ref|Reference transcript length|words;WER|Word error ratio|dimensionless',
'N at least W, no padding or centering for frame arithmetic; fixed whitespace-normalized reference words for WER. No audio data is uploaded or speech model run by this lesson.',
'Why is the WER denominator three in the example?', ['There are three reference words, irrespective of hypothesis length.','There are three correctly generated audio frames.','WER always divides by the number of hypothesis words.'],
'Feature compression reduces sequence length but discards waveform detail. Transcript metrics can miss semantic severity and conceal speaker or noise failures.')
add('denoising-generative-objectives','Compute a noisy training target and distinguish noise regression from a flow vector field.',
'Generative training can operate on continuous values rather than discrete next-token labels. In a DDPM noise-prediction objective, a clean example is mixed with Gaussian noise at a chosen level and the model predicts that noise. Generation later runs a specified reverse sampling process; a single good denoising prediction is not a complete generated sample. Flow matching instead regresses a vector field along a declared probability path. The target, time convention and sampler therefore differ even when both start from noise. Neither mechanism is implemented by the tiny causal decoder.',
'Specify whether time indexes a discrete diffusion level or a continuous path. || Construct the noisy/interpolated input and exact regression target from retained random values. || Compute the declared coordinate-sum or coordinate-mean loss. || Evaluate samples from a complete stated sampler with fixed seeds and quality metrics separate from regression loss.',
'For DDPM-style training, x0=2, cumulative alpha=0.64 and sampled noise epsilon=−1 give xt=0.8×2+0.6×(−1)=1. A noise prediction −0.5 has squared error 0.25. In a separate straight flow path from noise x0=−1 to data x1=2, time t=0.25 gives xt=−0.25 and target velocity x1−x0=3. The noise target −1 and flow target 3 refer to different constructions.',
 r'x_t=\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0+\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\epsilon,\quad L_\epsilon=\lVert\epsilon-\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)\rVert^2,\qquad x_t^{flow}=(1-t)x_0+t x_1,\quad u=x_1-x_0',
'x_0|Clean diffusion value or explicitly named flow start|dimensionless;x_1|Data endpoint in the flow example|dimensionless;alpha bar_t|Cumulative diffusion signal factor|dimensionless;epsilon|Sampled standard Gaussian noise|dimensionless;t|Declared diffusion index or normalized flow time|index or dimensionless;u|Straight-path target velocity|value per normalized time;L_epsilon|Coordinate-summed noise error|squared value',
'One-dimensional authored samples; diffusion and flow use separate time/start conventions explicitly. No complete sampler, likelihood bound or image-quality benchmark is executed.',
'Why are the two regression targets different?', ['They supervise different declared paths and predicted quantities.','All noise-based objectives must have the same target.','The flow velocity is the model’s next-token probability.'],
'Continuous objectives offer different representation and sampling choices. More sampling steps incur additional work but do not alone guarantee useful or faithful outputs.')
add('video-latent-prediction','Compare masked feature prediction with pixel generation and test temporal information use.',
'Video prediction can target learned representations of hidden regions rather than reconstruct every pixel. A target encoder and a predictor have different roles; a stop-gradient or moving-average target prevents treating the target as an unconstrained jointly changing answer. V-JEPA 2 describes masked video representation prediction before adding action-conditioned training. That observational stage does not by itself specify what a robot action will cause. Shuffling time or removing motion cues provides a useful control for whether temporal information matters. The scalar example below illustrates target accounting rather than executing a learned video encoder.',
'Mark visible context and hidden target positions without leaking target features into the predictor input. || Record how the target encoder is updated and where gradients stop. || Compare predicted and target features under the declared norm and denominator. || Test temporal order and held-out motion, then evaluate a downstream task separately from feature loss.',
'An authored target representation for two masked frames is [2,4], while the predictor emits [1.5,3]. Mean absolute feature error is (0.5+1)/2=0.75. Predicting [2,4] gives zero for these two scalar targets, yet says nothing about unseen texture or whether a control action is correct. A moving-average target coefficient 0.9 combines old parameter 2 and current online parameter 3 into 2.1.',
 r'L=\frac1M\sum_{j\in mask}|\hat z_j-\operatorname{sg}(z_j)|,\qquad \bar\theta^+=\rho\bar\theta+(1-\rho)\theta',
'L|Mean absolute masked-feature error|feature units;M|Scored scalar feature coordinates|coordinates;z_j,hat z_j|Target and predicted representation coordinates|feature units;sg|Stop-gradient operation|operator;theta,bar theta|Online and target-encoder parameters|parameters;rho|Moving-average coefficient|dimensionless',
'Scalar authored feature targets and a declared L1 coordinate mean. Worked EMA arithmetic does not replicate an encoder, dataset or the full V-JEPA 2 training recipe.',
'What does zero error on the two representation targets establish?', ['Those two target coordinates were reproduced under the stated representation.','Every future pixel is realistic.','The model can control any robot.'],
'Latent prediction can focus learning on retained features while discarding details. Features useful for one downstream task may omit another task’s needed information.')
add('action-conditioned-world-model','Change an action while holding state fixed and compare predicted transitions with a held-out environment.',
'An action-conditioned world model predicts consequences from state and action inputs. A visually plausible continuation from observational video is not sufficient evidence that the model distinguishes interventions. Training requires action-linked transitions with a stated coordinate system and observation timing. One-step prediction error can be small while errors compound during repeated predicted steps, especially when a policy seeks states absent from training. V-JEPA 2-AC and Dreamer use different learned-state mechanisms; the worked scalar transition below isolates their shared action-dependence requirement without replicating either.',
'Retain state, executed action and next observation as one time-aligned transition. || Fit or inspect a predictor that receives the action explicitly. || Hold initial state fixed and compare alternative action predictions. || Evaluate one-step and multi-step errors separately on held-out trajectories and policy-induced states.',
'In an authored environment x_next=x+2a, x=1 with action +0.5 yields 2 and action −0.5 yields 0. A model x_hat_next=x+1.8a predicts 1.9 and 0.1, giving absolute error 0.1 each. Repeating action +0.5 for five predicted steps from x=1 gives model state 5.5 versus true state 6: error grows to 0.5. This exact linear example demonstrates accumulation, not a universal growth law.',
 r'\hat x_{t+1}=f_\theta(\hat x_t,a_t),\qquad e_H=|\hat x_{t+H}-x_{t+H}|',
'x,hat x|Observed and predicted scalar position in the example|m;a|Declared normalized action command|dimensionless;f_theta|Learned or supplied transition predictor|function;t,H|Transition index and rollout horizon|steps;e_H|H-step position error|m',
'Example gain is 2 m per normalized command per step; model gain is 1.8. No clipping, noise or hidden state. This is worked arithmetic, not a trained world-model experience.',
'What distinguishes action-conditioned prediction from an action-free continuation?', ['Changing only the action can change the predicted next state.','The generated scene merely looks realistic.','Every future state is already included in the input.'],
'Action data supports control-relevant prediction but is limited by collected interventions and embodiment. A policy can exploit errors beyond that support.')
add('imagined-rollouts-policy-learning','Compare a policy’s imagined return with return in the actual environment and identify model exploitation.',
'A learned world model can supply imagined transitions for training an actor and critic. The actor then chooses actions and the critic estimates future returns. Training with imagined trajectories is different from running an online search at every real decision. Dreamer’s actor can act from its learned state without a fresh tree search; model-predictive control uses a different selection procedure. Both depend on how well the learned dynamics and reward model represent the states the policy visits. Optimizing an imperfect model can select actions that look excellent only inside that model.',
'Start imagined trajectories from recorded states with a stated model identity. || Roll actions through predicted transitions and rewards, respecting termination. || Train or compare policies under a declared return objective. || Execute the selected policy in the separate environment and record the gap, including states where the model was unsupported.',
'For two future rewards [1,2] and discount 0.9, return is 1+0.9×2=2.8. In an authored choice, a safe action has imagined and actual return 2, while an unsupported action has imagined return 5 and actual return −1. Maximizing imagined return selects the latter, with a model-return gap of 6. A better score in the model therefore can accompany a worse real task outcome.',
 r'G_t=\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\gamma^k r_{t+k},\qquad \Delta_{model}=\hat G-G_{environment}',
'G_t|Finite-horizon discounted return|reward units;gamma|Discount factor|dimensionless;H|Reward horizon|steps;r|Reward at the stated transition boundary|reward units;hat G|Return predicted by the model|reward units;Delta_model|Predicted minus environment return|reward units',
'Authored deterministic rewards and finite horizon; no learned actor/critic or robot is executed here. Terminal states must stop or otherwise explicitly zero further rewards.',
'Why does the unsupported action look best during imagined optimization?', ['Its predicted return is overestimated relative to the separate environment.','Imagined and actual returns are identical by definition.','Discounting guarantees the model cannot be exploited.'],
'Imagined experience can reduce real interactions but inherits model error. Shorter imagination reduces some error accumulation while limiting long-horizon credit assignment.')
add('vision-language-action-interface','Decode action tokens into physical commands with explicit frame, units and validity checks.',
'A vision-language-action interface produces commands for an environment, not merely descriptive text. Discrete action tokens require an inverse mapping into physical quantities, including coordinate frame, units, control rate and bounds. The OpenVLA paper uses 256 bins per action dimension over training-data quantile ranges; this is a recipe-specific encoding rather than a universal actuator protocol. Quantization error and out-of-range clipping require separate accounting. A syntactically valid token can still request an unsafe or meaningless command if its frame or scale is mismatched.',
'Declare the action dimension, reference frame, units, control period and allowed range. || Quantize a training action under one stated binning convention. || Decode to the chosen representative and measure physical error. || Reject invalid coordinates, stale observations or incompatible actuator contracts before execution; a model probability is not authorization.',
'An authored four-bin position command spans [−1,1] m with equal width 0.5 m and midpoint decoding. Centers are [−0.75,−0.25,0.25,0.75] m. Command 0.3 m maps to bin 2 (zero-based) and decodes to 0.25 m, error −0.05 m. Interior midpoint error is at most 0.25 m. A 2 m command is outside the declared range; silently clipping it loses 1.25 m relative to the top center and should not be reported as ordinary quantization error.',
 r'\Delta=\frac{u-l}{K},\quad b=\left\lfloor\frac{a-l}{\Delta}\right\rfloor,\quad \hat a=l+(b+\tfrac12)\Delta',
'l,u|Lower and upper declared action limits|m;K|Equal-width bins|bins;Delta|Bin width|m;b|Zero-based bin index, with endpoint convention|index;a,hat a|Command and midpoint reconstruction|m',
'Authored four-bin midpoint convention, range checked first; a=u maps to final bin. It is not a verbatim implementation of OpenVLA token IDs or quantile calibration.',
'Why must a 2 m command be handled separately?', ['It lies outside the declared encoding range and invalidates the interior error bound.','Every token automatically authorizes any physical motion.','Quantization error is always zero for model outputs.'],
'Discrete tokens reuse sequence-model interfaces but introduce resolution, range and control-contract constraints. Textual plausibility does not establish valid actuation.')
add('diffusion-action-horizon','Separate generated action horizon, executed prefix and replanning interval.',
'A diffusion action policy generates a sequence of future commands conditioned on observations. Receding-horizon execution applies only a prefix, then receives new observations and generates another sequence. The prediction horizon and action-execution horizon are therefore different parameters. Executing a longer prefix reduces replanning frequency but delays reactions to disturbances. Inference latency also consumes part of the control budget and must be measured or explicitly assumed. Diffusion Policy studies this structure; the scalar command sequence here is a worked control trace, not a trained denoising policy.',
'Record the observation time and generate an action chunk under a declared policy identity. || Execute only the selected prefix with an explicit command/period convention. || Observe disturbances and update the next planning state. || Compare closed-loop and open-loop outcomes while separating assumed inference delay from actual transition arithmetic.',
'For x_next=x+a, start x=0 and an authored chunk [1,1,1,1] targeting x=4. After executing the first command, a disturbance adds +1, so observed x=2. Replanning with [1,1,0,0] and executing the next two commands reaches 4. Executing all three remaining original commands instead reaches 5. At an assumed 10 Hz command rate, a two-command prefix covers 0.2 s; an assumed 0.05 s inference delay is one quarter of that interval, not a measured policy latency.',
 r'T_{prefix}=\frac{H_a}{f},\qquad x_{t+1}=x_t+a_t+d_t',
'T_prefix|Time covered by executed action prefix|s;H_a|Executed commands before replanning|commands;f|Command rate|commands per s;x_t|Position|m;a_t|Position increment command|m per step;d_t|Disturbance at the declared step|m',
'Authored deterministic scalar integrator and command chunks. Rate and delay are stated assumptions; no diffusion inference or physical robot timing is measured.',
'What changes when only a prefix is executed before observing again?', ['The next commands can be replanned from the disturbed state.','The original chunk becomes guaranteed correct.','The policy’s weights must be retrained after every command.'],
'Frequent replanning improves responsiveness but spends more inference work. Long prefixes tolerate delay while increasing open-loop exposure to model and environment error.')
def deep(*args,**kwargs):
 add(*args,**kwargs); id=args[0]; enrich[id]=rows.pop(id)
deep('training-data','Audit data lineage at the family and split boundaries.',
'Advanced lifecycle practice retains source identity, transformation history, tokenizer version and document-family split assignment together. A train/development/final separation is meaningful only if near-identical families do not cross it. The executable symbol corpus records these fields, while its source is original synthetic material rather than a crawl or human conversation. Development answers support iteration; the first final report records the evaluated checkpoint and cannot be silently rewritten after another update.',
'Preserve raw-source and transformed-example identities. || Assign whole document families to splits before tokenization-dependent sampling. || Record final exposure as part of the experimental history, including after importing an older checkpoint.',
'The executable corpus has 12 training, 8 development and 4 final examples per domain. Across copy and swap this is 24/16/8 example records, but only 12/8/4 distinct prompt families because each domain assigns its own answer to the same family. Treating all 48 records as independent prompt families would double-count the evidence.',
 r'N_{records}=\sum_s N_s,\qquad N_{families}=|\{\operatorname{family}(x):x\in D\}|',
'N_records|Stored example rows|examples;N_s|Rows in split s|examples;N_families|Distinct prompt-family identities|families;D|Declared corpus|dataset',
'Two intentionally conflicting domains share prompt families within the same split, never across splits. No external dataset license or privacy review is implied.',
'Why are there only 24 distinct prompt families across the 48 records?', ['Each prompt family is represented once in copy and once in swap.','Half the records are secretly final-test examples.','Every source record is necessarily an independent task.'],
'Fine-grained lineage requires more metadata but prevents apparently separate records from overstating independent coverage.', 'executable corpus lineage')
deep('tokenizer','Distinguish fitting a vocabulary from using a fixed token mapping on held-out input.',
'Advanced tokenization work treats merge learning as a data-dependent training step. Merge counts must come from training text, with a deterministic tie rule and an explicit fallback for unseen pieces. Encoding a held-out string with already learned merges is inference; updating merges from that string changes the tokenizer and leaks held-out information. The lifecycle core intentionally uses a fixed nine-symbol whitespace vocabulary, so it does not simulate BPE fitting.',
'Count adjacent symbol pairs only in the training corpus under the boundary convention. || Select the most frequent pair with a declared tie rule and apply merges consistently. || Freeze the learned tokenizer before encoding development and final text.',
'Use training words “aa” twice and “ab” once, represented as character sequences with no cross-word pairs. Pair (a,a) has count 2 and (a,b) count 1, so the first merge forms aa. Encoding held-out “aab” yields [aa,b] under this toy merge list. Letting held-out “ab” occurrences change the winning pair would change both input IDs and evaluation token denominators.',
 r'(u^*,v^*)=\arg\max_{(u,v)}\sum_{w\in D_{train}}c_w(u,v)',
'u,v|Adjacent current symbols or subwords|symbols;c_w|Adjacent-pair occurrences in training item w|count;D_train|Tokenizer fitting corpus|dataset',
'Toy word boundaries and deterministic lexical tie rule; no byte fallback or production BPE trainer is executed by the core.',
'Which operation would leak held-out information into the tokenizer?', ['Counting held-out pairs when choosing the next merge.','Applying already frozen merges to held-out text.','Recording the frozen vocabulary identity.'],
'More merges can shorten encoded sequences while changing rare-piece coverage and making tokenizer identity essential for checkpoint compatibility.')
deep('embedding','Accumulate gradients into shared lookup rows and distinguish tied from separate output weights.',
'A lookup row is a shared parameter whenever the same token occurs more than once. Reverse-mode gradients from every occurrence must accumulate into that row. If input embeddings and the output head are tied, the output path adds another contribution to the same parameters. The executable lifecycle model uses separate matrices, so tying is a worked extension rather than an implicit property of all decoders.',
'Identify all input positions that read the same token row. || Add each position’s backward contribution at matching feature coordinates. || If weights are explicitly tied, add output-path gradients before the optimizer update.',
'Token a appears twice and receives row gradients [1,2] and [3,−1]. Its input-embedding gradient is [4,1]. A separate token b receives [0.5,0.5] and must not be added to a. If an explicitly tied output path contributes [−2,1] to a, the combined tied gradient becomes [2,2]. The core instead keeps the output contribution in its separate unembedding tensor.',
 r'\frac{\partial L}{\partial E_{v,:}}=\sum_{t:x_t=v}\frac{\partial L}{\partial e_t}+g^{tied\ output}_{v,:}',
'E_v|Shared embedding row for token v|dimensionless;e_t|Lookup result at input position t|dimensionless;g|Derivative contribution, zero here unless weights are explicitly tied|loss per parameter;x_t|Token identity|identifier;L|Scalar objective|loss',
'Matching coordinates, summed path derivatives and an explicit tied/separate convention. Core weights are separate.',
'What is the input-only gradient for the repeated a row?', ['[4,1].','[3,−1], because the second occurrence replaces the first.','[4.5,1.5], because every token shares one row.'],
'Sharing reduces independent parameters and pools evidence across occurrences, but requires correct gradient accumulation and a deliberate tying convention.', 'executable lookup gradients; worked tying')
deep('attention','Trace a numerical softmax derivative through a causal value mixture.',
'Attention learning differentiates both the value path and the probabilities controlling that mixture. A causal mask removes future positions from the allowed softmax support; it is not merely a visual annotation. The executable decoder exposes projected Q/K/V, scores, probabilities and the complete gradient graph. A small analytical example clarifies why changing one score can redistribute weight across all allowed values.',
'Form scores only for positions the query may attend to. || Normalize those scores together and mix their value vectors. || Backpropagate through both softmax probabilities and value projections; verify prefix invariance under future-token changes.',
'With allowed scores [0,ln3] and scalar values [2,6], attention probabilities are [0.25,0.75] and output is 5. For the output itself as the scalar objective, derivative with respect to score one is 0.25×(2−5)=−0.75 and score two is 0.75×(6−5)=0.75. At the first causal position only value 2 is allowed, so output is 2 regardless of future score or value.',
 r'o=\sum_j a_jv_j,\qquad \frac{\partial o}{\partial s_j}=a_j(v_j-o)',
'o|Scalar attention output in this example|value units;a_j|Softmax probability over allowed keys|dimensionless;v_j|Allowed scalar value|value units;s_j|Pre-softmax score|dimensionless',
'Scalar value example, softmax over the allowed set, values held fixed for the shown score derivative. Full model also differentiates values and projections.',
'What is the derivative of output with respect to the first score?', ['−0.75.','+0.75.','Zero merely because attention probabilities sum to one.'],
'Adaptive mixing provides context dependence while making masks and shared softmax normalization part of the learning contract.', 'executable causal attention gradients')
deep('feed-forward','Count a nonlinear block’s learned state and differentiate its activation.',
'A feed-forward sublayer transforms features independently at each token position using shared weights. Nonlinearity is what prevents two linear maps from collapsing into one equivalent linear map. Activation choice changes derivative behavior and parameter convention; a tanh block is not a ReLU or gated SwiGLU block. The lifecycle core uses tanh and explicit biases, all included in its parameter ledger.',
'Compute the expanded affine features including bias. || Apply the declared nonlinearity coordinate by coordinate. || Project back to model width and accumulate shared weight gradients across tokens.',
'For scalar h=tanh(wx+b), x=2, w=0 and b=0 give h=0 and dh/dw=(1−h²)x=2. At a saturated activation h≈0.99 the same derivative is about 0.0398. In the core, a 6→12→6 tanh block with both biases has 6×12+12+12×6+6=162 learned parameters. Its behavior must be evaluated through actual losses, not inferred from that count.',
 r'h=\tanh(wx+b),\qquad \frac{\partial h}{\partial w}=(1-h^2)x,\qquad P=dh+h+hd+d',
'h|Tanh output or, in the parameter ledger, explicitly stated hidden width|dimensionless or features;w,b|Scalar weight and bias|dimensionless;x|Scalar input|dimensionless;d|Model width in ledger|features;P|Two affine maps plus biases|parameters',
'The scalar activation symbol h and hidden-width ledger are explicitly separated by context. Core uses tanh, not the original Transformer ReLU or a gated variant.',
'Why does the derivative shrink near h=0.99?', ['The tanh derivative 1−h² is close to zero.','The optimizer automatically freezes every feature.','Two linear maps are always equivalent to a nonlinear one.'],
'Expansion and nonlinearity add representational choices and activation state; saturation and scale affect the available local gradient.', 'executable nonlinear block')
deep('residual-normalization','Distinguish an identity gradient path from normalization’s scale transformation.',
'A residual addition passes an input directly to the output alongside a learned transformation, creating an identity term in the derivative. Normalization modifies feature scale before or after a sublayer according to the declared architecture. RMS normalization does not subtract the mean, and its learned gain and epsilon conventions must be stated. The lifecycle variant uses pre-RMS normalization with fixed gain one and epsilon 1e-5; it is not exactly the original Transformer normalization placement.',
'Trace the identity branch separately from the sublayer branch. || Compute mean square over the declared feature axis, with epsilon placed explicitly. || Compare actual activation and gradient values rather than assuming a residual guarantees stable learning.',
'For y=x+f(x), local derivative is 1+f′(x); if f′(x)=−0.2 the total is 0.8. For vector [3,4], RMS without epsilon is sqrt((9+16)/2)=sqrt12.5=3.535534, giving normalized values [0.848528,1.131371]. Their mean is not zero. The core’s positive epsilon changes these numbers slightly and prevents division by zero.',
 r'\frac{dy}{dx}=1+f\prime(x),\qquad \operatorname{RMSNorm}(x)_i=\frac{x_i}{\sqrt{d^{-1}\sum_j x_j^2+\epsilon}}',
'x_i|Input feature|dimensionless;d|Features normalized together|features;epsilon|Positive stabilizer in mean-square units|dimensionless squared;f|Residual sublayer|function;y|Residual output|dimensionless',
'Fixed gain one; epsilon zero only for the explicitly labeled hand calculation. Core epsilon is 1e-5, so exact scale invariance is qualified.',
'Does RMS normalization force the output mean to zero?', ['No; it rescales by root mean square without subtracting the mean.','Yes, for every input.','Only when residual paths are absent.'],
'Residuals and normalization change gradient and scale behavior but do not guarantee convergence under arbitrary data, depth or learning rate.', 'executable residual and RMS paths')
deep('output-head','Connect logits, likelihood gradients and sampling without turning confidence into truth.',
'The output head supplies logits for a probability distribution. Negative log likelihood compares that distribution with the observed target and differentiates to probability minus target indicator. Greedy decoding and categorical sampling consume the same probabilities differently. Neither updates the model’s weights by itself. The lifecycle generator retains a separate inference RNG so that requesting a sampled answer cannot change later training draws.',
'Apply stable log-softmax to all vocabulary logits. || Gather the target probability and compute the logit derivative. || Select greedy or seeded categorical output, keeping generation state separate from optimizer state.',
'For logits [0,ln2,ln3], probabilities are [1/6,1/3,1/2]. With target class three, loss is ln2=0.693147 and gradient is [1/6,1/3,−1/2]. Greedy decoding chooses class three, while one categorical sample can choose another class. A sample different from the greedy class is not evidence that the probabilities or gradient were incorrectly computed.',
 r'p_i=\frac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_j e^{z_j}},\qquad L=-\log p_y,\qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial z_i}=p_i-\mathbf1[i=y]',
'z_i|Vocabulary logit|dimensionless;p_i|Next-token probability|dimensionless;y|Target class|index;L|One target negative log likelihood|nats',
'Finite logits, natural logarithms and one categorical target. Stable implementation subtracts maximum logit; that shift preserves the distribution.',
'Why can a categorical sample differ from the greedy token?', ['Sampling follows the whole distribution, including lower-probability outcomes.','Sampling performs a hidden optimizer update.','The largest logit is never used in sampling.'],
'Sampling can provide output diversity while increasing decision variance. Probability quality and task correctness require their own evaluation.', 'executable output probabilities and generation')
deep('moe','Calculate routing loads and a declared auxiliary loss separately from expert capacity.',
'An MoE router assigns tokens to selected experts, which changes both active computation and where gradients flow. A load-balancing auxiliary loss encourages use of experts under a particular formula; it is distinct from a hard capacity limit or dropping policy. The Switch top-one convention uses discrete dispatch fractions and mean router probabilities. Its dispatch counts are not differentiated through argmax. The lifecycle model is dense and does not execute expert routing or this auxiliary loss.',
'Route tokens under an explicit top-k and tie convention. || Count dispatch fractions and mean router probabilities separately. || Evaluate the chosen auxiliary formula and identify its differentiable path. || Apply expert capacity and account for accepted, dropped or bypassed tokens independently.',
'Six tokens dispatch to three experts with counts [3,2,1], so f=[1/2,1/3,1/6]. Mean router probabilities are P=[1/2,1/3,1/6]. With alpha=0.01 and N=3, auxiliary loss is 0.01×3×(1/4+1/9+1/36)=0.011667. Uniform f=P=[1/3,1/3,1/3] gives 0.01. A hard capacity of two tokens per expert still rejects or bypasses one token from expert one under a declared policy.',
 r'L_{aux}=\alpha N\sum_i f_iP_i,\quad f_i=\frac1T\sum_x\mathbf1[\arg\max_j p_j(x)=i],\quad P_i=\frac1T\sum_xp_i(x)',
'L_aux|Switch-style auxiliary objective|dimensionless;alpha|Auxiliary coefficient|dimensionless;N|Experts|experts;T|Routed tokens|tokens;f_i|Discrete dispatch fraction|dimensionless;P_i|Mean router probability|dimensionless;p_i(x)|Router probability for token x|dimensionless',
'Switch top-one auxiliary convention, before capacity handling. Discrete f is treated as nondifferentiable; P supplies a gradient path. Worked-only routing.',
'Does an auxiliary loss alone enforce the hard capacity of two?', ['No; dispatch capacity and overflow behavior need a separate rule.','Yes; auxiliary losses physically prevent every overflow.','Only if all model parameters are dense.'],
'Balancing promotes resource use but can compete with specialization. Capacity rules can discard or bypass tokens even when the average router probabilities look reasonable.')
deep('loss-optimizer','Verify the first actual gradient-driven SGD and Adam updates under explicit state.',
'An optimizer consumes a derived gradient; a supplied-gradient calculator teaches only the last part of that chain. The lifecycle experience adds an actual decoder loss and reverse pass before applying either SGD or Adam. Adam’s moments and step counter are learning state, not display history. Global norm clipping occurs before those moments. Changing the objective changes the gradient even if the optimizer setting is held fixed.',
'Compute the gradient from the current batch loss and retain its denominator. || Apply the declared global clipping rule. || Update moments, bias-correct them and apply the chosen learning rate; save all resulting state.',
'For a scalar gradient g=0.5, weight 2 and learning rate 0.1, SGD gives 1.95. For the first Adam step with zero moments, beta1=0.9 and beta2=0.999, m=0.05 and v=0.00025; bias-corrected values are 0.5 and 0.25. With epsilon=1e-8 the Adam result is approximately 1.900000002. It differs from SGD because Adam normalizes by the second-moment scale.',
 r'm_t=\beta_1m_{t-1}+(1-\beta_1)g_t,\quad v_t=\beta_2v_{t-1}+(1-\beta_2)g_t^2,\quad\theta^+=\theta-\eta\frac{m_t/(1-\beta_1^t)}{\sqrt{v_t/(1-\beta_2^t)}+\epsilon}',
'm_t|First gradient moment|gradient units;v_t|Second gradient moment|squared gradient units;g_t|Gradient after declared clipping|gradient units;beta_1,beta_2|Moment decay factors|dimensionless;t|Optimizer update count|updates;eta|Learning rate|dimensionless;epsilon|Denominator stabilizer|gradient units;theta|Parameter|dimensionless',
'No weight decay or AMSGrad; elementwise squares/division. First-step worked values use an unclipped scalar gradient of 0.5.',
'Why is the first Adam change about 0.1 rather than 0.05?', ['The bias-corrected gradient is divided by its second-moment root.','Adam ignores gradients and always chooses random weights.','SGD and Adam use identical state equations.'],
'Adaptive state changes update scaling and must survive a reproducible resume. It does not replace a correct derivative or suitable data.', 'executable derived-gradient optimizer')
deep('checkpoint','Resume weights, optimizer, RNG and data cursor as one experiment state.',
'A full learning checkpoint must carry enough state to reproduce the next update. Weights-only files can reproduce a forward function while failing to reproduce training, because moments, random draws and data position differ. The lifecycle record also includes tokenizer/data/model versions, reference snapshots, stage contract and final-test exposure. Import validation checks compatibility and bounded sizes before replacing a current run. Its compact runtime fingerprint is an identifier for teaching, not an authenticated signature.',
'Serialize weights, moments, optimizer counter, training RNG, draw cursor and complete configuration. || Validate versions, finite values, shapes and cross-field invariants before committing an import. || Resume from the saved cursor and compare every subsequent state field with an uninterrupted reference.',
'An automated fixture trains 17 updates, exports the complete checkpoint, restores it and performs 23 more. Its weights, moments, RNG, draw counter and history exactly match 40 uninterrupted updates in the same deterministic binary64 implementation. With two sampled examples per update, the final draw count is 80. Restoring only weights while resetting moments produces a different next trajectory.',
 r'S_{t+1}=F(S_t,D_t),\qquad S_t=(\theta_t,m_t,v_t,t,RNG_t,cursor_t,config)',
'S_t|Complete reproducible learning state|record;F|Declared deterministic update given its saved state|function;D_t|Sampled training data selected by saved RNG/cursor policy|examples;theta,m,v|Weights and optimizer moment arrays|parameters and moments;t|Optimizer counter|updates',
'Exact equality is tested within one numerical implementation and operation order. Cross-device floating-point kernels can require a tolerance or different reproducibility contract.',
'What is missing from a weights-only continuation record?', ['Optimizer and sampling state needed to reproduce the next training update.','The ability to compute any forward prediction.','Only an optional chart title.'],
'Complete state improves reproducibility but enlarges records and compatibility obligations. An imported record is not authenticated empirical evidence.', 'executable full checkpoint')
deep('training-memory-state','Inventory real trainable arrays separately from activation and runtime overhead.',
'Training memory includes persistent parameters and optimizer arrays plus temporary gradients and saved forward intermediates. Their lifetimes differ. A coefficient payload calculation is useful only when it names precision and excluded structures. Activation rematerialization trades recomputation for retained intermediates, but is not implemented merely because a UI shows an estimated memory total. The lifecycle core exposes actual scalar tape-node counts and parameter arrays; those nodes are not hardware FLOPs or a byte-accurate JavaScript heap measurement.',
'Count persistent weight and moment arrays under the chosen numerical representation. || Separate the current gradient, frozen reference and saved activations from those arrays. || Use a lifetime trace or measured heap evidence before claiming peak runtime memory.',
'The core has 423 binary64 weights, requiring 3384 raw bytes. Two Adam moment arrays add 6768 bytes, so weights plus moments total 10152. Adding one equally sized gradient vector yields 13536 raw bytes. A frozen reference adds another 3384. These sums exclude the scalar tape’s graph objects, history, JSON encoding, React state and allocator overhead.',
 r'B_{payload}=P\sum_k b_k',
'B_payload|Payload of the explicitly inventoried full-length arrays|bytes;P|Parameters represented in every listed array|parameters;b_k|Bytes per parameter for listed array k|bytes per parameter',
'423 entries per full array, eight bytes per binary64 number; no claim that JavaScript object storage equals this payload. No rematerialization feature is executed.',
'What does 10152 bytes include?', ['Weights and the two full Adam moment arrays only.','The complete browser heap at peak training.','All future checkpoint JSON and activation history.'],
'Persistent-state formulas are easy to inspect but omit dynamic lifetimes. Lower raw precision or rematerialization requires separate numerical and execution contracts.', 'executable arrays; payload arithmetic')
deep('scaling-laws','Interpret a fitted loss surface under a bounded compute and evaluation regime.',
'A scaling relation is an empirical fit with a metric, training recipe and observed range. It does not turn parameter count or compute into a universal capability guarantee. Compute-optimal allocation asks how a fixed budget is divided between model size and training data under the study’s cost model. The lifecycle’s one fixed architecture and short runs do not estimate frontier scaling exponents. Comparing several actual runs would require controlled token budgets, held-out fitting checks and residuals rather than a smooth curve drawn from assumptions.',
'Name the fitted loss metric, tokenizer and data mixture. || Separate parameter/data budgets from measured or modeled computation. || Hold evaluation out of fitting and report residuals and extrapolation boundaries.',
'For an authored fit L(N,D)=1+4/sqrt(N)+2/sqrt(D), N=16,D=16 gives 2.5. Doubling both to 32 gives 2.060660. This is arithmetic from chosen coefficients, not measured learning. Under the illustrative dense-training cost C=6ND, the two settings cost 1536 and 6144 modeled operations, a fourfold ratio. A real fit requires observed runs and a justified operation convention.',
 r'L(N,D)=E+AN^{-\alpha}+BD^{-\beta},\qquad C\approx6ND',
'L|Fitted held-out target loss|nats per token;N|Trainable parameter count under the fitted convention|parameters;D|Training token presentations|tokens;E,A,B|Fitted coefficients with units consistent with N and D|fit coefficients;alpha,beta|Fitted positive exponents|dimensionless;C|Approximate dense training work under the chosen convention|FLOPs',
'Illustrative coefficients only. The 6ND heuristic omits architecture-specific terms and is not a measured cost of the scalar-tape core.',
'What would justify calling the displayed relation an empirical fit?', ['Observed controlled runs, fitting procedure and held-out residual checks.','Choosing coefficients that make a smooth curve.','One successful 423-parameter run alone.'],
'Fitted trends aid budget reasoning within their evidence range. Extrapolation across architectures, data and objectives can be misleading.')
deep('post-training','Choose a supervision signal and objective independently of the parameterization.',
'Post-training encompasses multiple contracts rather than one universal loss. Demonstration targets support supervised likelihood training; labeled response pairs support preference objectives; a learned reward or a checker supplies a signal for sampled policy updates. The same decoder can undergo several of these stages while keeping its architecture. LoRA or full tuning specifies which parameters may change, not whether the signal is SFT, DPO or reward. The executable lifecycle includes one-turn SFT, DPO and a bounded verifier bandit, with exact scope shown beside each branch.',
'Identify whether the available evidence is a demonstration, pair label or scored sampled action. || State the exact loss and target/pair/rollout denominator. || Declare trainable state and the frozen reference separately from the objective.',
'For a batch of two one-turn demonstrations with answer plus EOS, SFT averages four target losses. Two DPO comparisons instead average two pair losses. The core reward branch samples four answers for each of two prompts and averages eight rollout terms. Their numerical loss values have different denominators and meanings; a lower DPO number is not directly better than a higher SFT number.',
 r'M_{SFT}=\sum_i T_i^{response},\qquad M_{DPO}=N_{pairs},\qquad M_{RL}=N_{prompts}G',
'M|Objective denominator for the named stage|targets pairs or rollouts;T_i^response|Included response targets in demonstration i|tokens;N_pairs|Preference comparisons|pairs;N_prompts|Sampled prompts|prompts;G|Actions sampled per prompt|rollouts per prompt',
'Core conventions: answer plus EOS SFT, response-pair DPO, one-action bandit rollouts. These are not a claim that every post-training implementation uses the same denominators.',
'Which comparison is invalid without further interpretation?', ['Ranking SFT and DPO quality by their raw scalar loss magnitudes.','Comparing the same task accuracy at both saved checkpoints.','Inspecting the response mask in SFT.'],
'Different signals address different behavior contracts and failure modes. Changing both signal and trainable state at once makes attribution harder.', 'executable SFT DPO and bandit')
deep('evaluation','Keep development feedback separate from a first final-test report and state its uncertainty.',
'Evaluation attaches a scoring rule and denominator to a fixed checkpoint. Development cases guide iteration, while an independent final test assesses a choice made before its answers are inspected. Repeatedly consulting that final set while choosing changes weakens its independence. A small perfect result also leaves substantial uncertainty about future cases. The lifecycle’s seal is procedural: source files remain inspectable, and its first final report retains checkpoint identity rather than updating silently as training continues.',
'Choose the task and score before revealing final outcomes. || Preserve checkpoint, dataset, tokenizer and inference identities with each result. || Report counts and uncertainty rather than only percentages; mark exposed final data as previously seen.',
'A result of 4/4 on the tiny final copy split is 100% observed accuracy, not proof of perfect population accuracy. A Wilson 95% interval with z=1.96 has lower bound about 0.510 and upper bound 1.000. The finite test supports only its declared toy task and split. Updating weights after the report changes model identity; the earlier 4/4 must remain attributed to the earlier checkpoint.',
 r'CI=\frac{\hat p+z^2/(2n)\ \pm\ z\sqrt{\frac{\hat p(1-\hat p)}{n}+\frac{z^2}{4n^2}}}{1+z^2/n}',
'CI|Wilson interval under the stated binomial model|probability interval;p hat|Observed success fraction|dimensionless;n|Evaluated cases|cases;z|Normal quantile, 1.96 for approximate 95 percent|dimensionless',
'Independent Bernoulli sampling model for the illustrative interval; the authored four-case split is not demonstrated to be a representative population sample. Interval does not cure dataset selection bias.',
'What should happen to a 4/4 report after the weights change?', ['It remains attached to the checkpoint that was actually evaluated.','It silently becomes a report about the new weights.','It proves every later model also succeeds.'],
'Sealing a final set preserves a useful protocol but limits iteration. Small or unrepresentative tests leave uncertainty even when all observed cases pass.', 'executable final report; worked uncertainty')
deep('tool-loop','Distinguish a proposed model action, validated execution and an observed successful outcome.',
'A model policy can propose a structured action, but a separate environment contract determines whether it is valid and authorized. The tool response becomes an observation, not an instruction with authority over the system. A plausible final sentence does not establish that the requested state transition occurred. The connected automation experience supplies bounded actions and verifiable outcomes; the lifecycle decoder itself generates symbols and does not call external tools or receive extra training from a tool response.',
'Log the policy input and proposed structured action. || Validate schema, capability, preconditions and budget before any state mutation. || Record the actual observation and independently score the desired state change.',
'In an authored inventory environment, stock starts at 3. A proposed reserve(2) passes a bound of available stock and commits stock=1. Retrying the same committed action with the same idempotency key returns its prior receipt rather than subtracting again. A fresh reserve(2) fails because only one remains. Saying “reserved” after that rejection is an answer failure even if its JSON syntax is valid.',
 r's_{t+1}=\begin{cases}T(s_t,a_t)&V(s_t,a_t)=1\\s_t&V(s_t,a_t)=0\end{cases}',
's_t|Environment state before validation|state;a_t|Proposed action with identity|record;V|Schema capability and precondition validator|Boolean;T|Authorized transition including idempotency semantics|function',
'Authored finite inventory contract, not an actual external order. Model generation, validator execution and learning updates have separate state boundaries.',
'What establishes successful reservation after a tool call?', ['A validated committed receipt and the corresponding environment state.','A fluent sentence saying the reservation succeeded.','The presence of a syntactically valid action proposal alone.'],
'Explicit validators can reject plausible but invalid proposals. Their rules must remain consistent with task intent and actual environment state.')
